           centered catch-light disc on ONE `::before`. TWO background layers compose:
           (TOP) the angle-keyed EDGE-anchored conic sweep — a warm-cream arc that
           brightens the RIM where the pointer's `--specular-angle` grazes (the
           glass-THICKNESS read iOS-27 fuses with the catch-light); (BOTTOM) the
           existing pointer-position centered disc. BOTH ride the SAME `--specular-
           intensity` cohort (the `opacity` below) + `plus-lighter` blend + warm-cream
           core `hsl(40 35% 92%)` — NEVER a second color/blend fork. The conic is masked
           to the rim band only (the `--glass-edge-glint-band` radial mask, scope 3),
           so the glint is a bounded RIM ring, NOT a whole-plate wash (the AX.W52 D19
           bounded-gleam discipline). The `--specular-angle` floor `0deg` sits the glint
           at the top edge when unwired (static rest — no ambient drift, no blowout). */
        /* BB.W-GLASS-ACCENT — the catch-light CORE COLOR composes the per-instance
           chromatic accent. The warm-cream `hsl(40 35% 92%)` core resolves through
           the ONE `--glass-specular-core` var, OKLab-mixed toward `var(--glass-
           accent)` at `var(--glass-accent-strength)`. This tints the CORE only — it
           is the COLOR axis; W-LENSING owns the GEOMETRY (the conic angle-keyed
           edge-glint + the radial disc + the masks + the `plus-lighter` blend, all
           BELOW, untouched). Tinting the ONE core tints BOTH the centered disc AND
           the rim glint (they share the core), so the data hue reads at the glint
           where the pointer grazes — the data-keyed colored catch-light. At
           `--glass-accent-strength: 0%` + `--glass-accent: transparent` (the
           `@property` defaults) the mix === `hsl(40 35% 92%)` byte-identical (the W2
           warm-cream floor). Each gradient stop reproduces its original alpha via
           `color-mix(in srgb, <core> N%, transparent)` (=== the prior `<core> / 0.NN`),
           so the geometry + falloff is byte-identical at rest. */
        --glass-specular-core: color-mix(
            in oklab,
            hsl(40 35% 92%),
            var(--glass-accent) var(--glass-accent-strength)
        );
        background:
            conic-gradient(
                from var(--specular-angle, 0deg) at 50% 50%,
                transparent 0deg,
                color-mix(in srgb, var(--glass-specular-core) 55%, transparent) 18deg,
                transparent 60deg,
                transparent 360deg
            ),
            radial-gradient(
                circle var(--glass-specular-size, 36%) at var(--specular-x, 50%) var(--specular-y, 50%),
                color-mix(in srgb, var(--glass-specular-core) 50%, transparent) 0%,
                transparent 70%,
                transparent 100%
            );
        opacity: var(--specular-intensity, 0);
        /* Per-layer masks (the `,`-separated list pairs with the two backgrounds in
           order): the EDGE conic is masked to the RIM BAND ONLY — transparent across
           the interior, opaque in the outer `--glass-edge-glint-band` (default 22%) — so
           the angle-keyed glint reads as a THICKNESS rim, not a center wash; the centered
           disc keeps its own-radius clip (AX.W52 D19). `mask-image` Baseline 2023-12-07;
           a non-supporting engine ignores it and the `inset: 0` + in-paint falloff still
           bound the gleam. */
        mask-image:
            radial-gradient(
                circle closest-side at 50% 50%,
                transparent calc(100% - var(--glass-edge-glint-band, 22%)),
                black calc(100% - calc(var(--glass-edge-glint-band, 22%) * 0.5)),
                black 100%
            ),
            radial-gradient(
                circle var(--glass-specular-size, 36%) at var(--specular-x, 50%) var(--specular-y, 50%),
                black 0%,
                black 60%,
                transparent 100%
            );
        /* AX.W52 D19 — `plus-lighter` (additive but HDR-clamped, Safari 16.4+) over
           the prior `screen` (a non-linear lightener that lifted the whole tile
           toward white — the muddy-card defect). A non-supporting engine degrades to
           a plain low-alpha warm overlay (the gleam composes normally — no blend, no
           blowout), the no-workaround floor; NOT a `screen` fallback. */
        mix-blend-mode: plus-lighter;
        /* The moving-specular TRANSITION is NOT declared here. An idle/unwired
           `::before` is STATIC (no interpolation track), so a bare glass rung / an
           un-armed Card / a glass Button at rest attaches ZERO animation tracks (the
           keyframes.js runtime counts 0 idle tracks, not 19). The transition rides
           ONLY the WIRED + interactive surfaces — the opt-in scoped block below. */
    }

    /* AY.W-GLASS — the moving-specular transition is OPT-IN (the Card wire-or-omit
       pattern, generalized). It attaches ONLY to a WIRED surface
       (`.glass-specular-track` — Card emits it conditionally, the dock controls + the
       Slider thumb compose it) OR to any glass surface under `:hover`/`:active` (the
       pointer is present, so the interpolation is legitimately live). An idle/unwired
       `.glass-floating` / `.glass-wash` / bare-rung `::before` carries NO transition
       and thus NO keyframes.js track. The §6 register is PRESERVED (still
       `--ease-standard` on the position-tracked + surface-opacity legs — H-motion §6
       \"position-tracked → --ease-standard\"; only the SELECTOR scope changes). The dock
       controls keep the always-on transition (they are interactive controls; their rest
       intensity is 0 so they paint nothing at rest, but a control legitimately carries
       the transition). */
    .glass-specular-track::before,
    .glass-material:hover::before,    .glass-material:active::before,
    .glass-wash:hover::before,        .glass-wash:active::before,
    .glass-quiet:hover::before,       .glass-quiet:active::before,
    .glass-resting:hover::before,     .glass-resting:active::before,
    .glass-floating:hover::before,    .glass-floating:active::before,
    .glass-overlay:hover::before,     .glass-overlay:active::before,
    .glass-card:hover::before,        .glass-card:active::before,
    .dock-icon-button::before,
    .dock-tab-button::before,
    .dock-select-trigger::before,
    .dock-dropdown-trigger::before {
        /* Smooth the position + intensity between pointer samples (the typed
           @property regs make the percentage stops interpolable). */
        transition:
            --specular-x var(--duration-fast, 150ms) var(--ease-standard, ease-out),
            --specular-y var(--duration-fast, 150ms) var(--ease-standard, ease-out),
            opacity var(--duration-normal, 240ms) var(--ease-standard, ease-out);
    }

    .dark .glass-material::before,
    .dark .glass-wash::before,
    .dark .glass-quiet::before,
    .dark .glass-resting::before,
    .dark .glass-floating::before,
    .dark .glass-overlay::before,
    .dark .glass-card::before,
    .dark .glass-specular-track::before,
    .dark .dock-icon-button::before,
    .dark .dock-tab-button::before,
    .dark .dock-select-trigger::before,
    .dark .dock-dropdown-trigger::before {
        /* Softer over the deep canvas — `plus-lighter` still lifts on dark, so the
           `.dark` cohort runs a lower hover/active rung (tokens.css §specular-
           intensity `.dark` arm); the rest rung is already 0 so the dark arm only
           softens hover/active via the cohort. The rest rung re-reads the (dark)
           token here so a dark `:root` override resolves. */
        --specular-intensity: var(--glass-specular-intensity-rest, 0);
    }

    /* (c) Interaction-light lockstep — hover/active lift the intensity in the
       SAME rungs the scale-press squish keys off, so material-light fires in
       lockstep with the press across every glass surface. Settles back on leave
       via the opacity transition above. */
    .glass-material:hover::before,
    .glass-wash:hover::before,
    .glass-quiet:hover::before,
    .glass-resting:hover::before,
    .glass-floating:hover::before,
    .glass-overlay:hover::before,
    .glass-card:hover::before,
    .glass-specular-track:hover::before,
    .dock-icon-button:hover::before,
    .dock-tab-button:hover::before,
    .dock-select-trigger:hover::before,
    .dock-dropdown-trigger:hover::before {
        --specular-intensity: var(--glass-specular-intensity-hover, 0.1);
    }
    .glass-material:active::before,
    .glass-wash:active::before,
    .glass-quiet:active::before,
    .glass-resting:active::before,
    .glass-floating:active::before,
    .glass-overlay:active::before,
    .glass-card:active::before,
    .glass-specular-track:active::before,
    .dock-icon-button:active::before,
    .dock-tab-button:active::before,
    .dock-select-trigger:active::before,
    .dock-dropdown-trigger:active::before {
        --specular-intensity: var(--glass-specular-intensity-active, 0.16);
    }

    /* AX.W45-TUNE C5 — the SELECTED dock control reads as the keyframes-dock glass
       register: a glass-tier active fill (`--dock-control-active-bg` → the floating
       plate, tokens.css) WITH the specular gleam waking over it, not a flat ink
       stamp. The CSS `:active` pseudo above only fires on press-down; a SELECTED tile
       (`.is-active` / `[aria-pressed]` / `[aria-expanded]` / `[aria-current]`) holds
       the gleam lit so the selected element reads as a lit glass tier the substrate
       shows through (the model the user named, `pass-3:13`). Family-scoped addition
       on the dock-control `::before`, line-disjoint from the shared recipe above. */
    .dock-icon-button:is(.is-active, .active, [aria-pressed=\"true\"], [aria-expanded=\"true\"], [aria-current=\"page\"])::before,
    .dock-tab-button:is(.is-active, .active, [aria-pressed=\"true\"], [aria-expanded=\"true\"], [aria-current=\"page\"])::before,
    .dock-select-trigger:is(.is-active, .active, [aria-pressed=\"true\"], [aria-expanded=\"true\"], [aria-current=\"page\"])::before,
    .dock-dropdown-trigger:is(.is-active, .active, [aria-pressed=\"true\"], [aria-expanded=\"true\"], [aria-current=\"page\"])::before {
        --specular-intensity: var(--glass-specular-intensity-active, 0.16);
    }

    /* BB.W-LENSING (scope 2) — the `:active` LENS-SWELL is RETIRED (DDR-LENS-BAKE,
       glass-refract.css header). The swell drove the displacement `scale` by mutating
       the `--glass-refract` axis on the `--glass-btn-press-t` press drive (28 rest → 44
       pressed). That depended on the var()-spliced head/`scale`/tail filter — which never
       PARSED as a `backdrop-filter` (three tokens: `url(\"…\") <n> url(\"…\")`), so the lens
       rendered `none` and the swell animated a value that reached NO filter. A CSS url()
       string token cannot carry a substituted `var()` (the engine does not re-tokenise
       inside a string), so a dynamic per-instance `scale` is structurally impossible in a
       single self-contained data-URI — and the single token is mandatory for both parse
       validity and bundler url()-rewrite safety. The `scale` is now BAKED at the §10
       default (28); the resting lens read is PRESERVED (and now actually renders), the
       press-depth animation is the deliberate tradeoff. The press squish + gleam lift on
       the SAME `--glass-btn-press-t` drive (material gleam, above) carry the press read. */

    /* ── BE.W-CLEAR-VARIANT — the Apple-Clear `surface=\"clear\"` decoration + the
       MANDATORY legibility scrim ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
       The 4th surface-axis member (`useSurfaceAxis.ts` maps `clear → glass-clear`).
       A `surface=\"clear\"` surface paints the maximally-translucent `--glass-bg-clear`
       (0.58 — the clearest rung the library ships, the album grid bleeds through) AND
       a MANDATORY legibility scrim IN THE SAME SEAM — a `.glass-clear` cannot exist
       without it (the Apple Clear contract: near-transparent → the text needs a scrim
       floor). The scrim DIMS THE BACKDROP (`color-mix(in srgb, var(--background) N%,
       transparent)` — the A5-1 modal-scrim idiom, painted BEHIND the translucent fill
       within the box, see the two-layer-background note below), DISTINCT from the W55
       `--glass-tint-*` PLATE-darken axis (the two legibility axes stay disjoint: the
       scrim dims the backdrop, the W55 tint darkens the plate). The scrim strength
       DERIVES from the sampled
       `--glass-backdrop-luma` (useGlassBackdropLuminance's existing write) so it dims
       MORE over a bright album grid (luma→1) and relaxes over a dark one (luma→0) —
       the dynamic legibility that betters Apple's fixed scrim. The `0.5` fallback is
       the safe mid-scrim when no observer is wired. The plate fill rides the SAME
       oklab-tint seam so the W55 darken reaches it; the see-through frost is the
       cross-engine FLOOR (pure opacity + backdrop-filter, no `url()` — Safari paints
       it via the build-time -webkit- prefix pass). The scrim is a STATIC paint (no
       layout, no animation — `proof:no-layout-animation` GREEN by construction).

       THE SCRIM IS A SECOND BACKGROUND LAYER, NOT A PSEUDO. The glass family already
       claims BOTH pseudos on every base rung — `::before` (the moving specular core)
       AND `::after` (the grain overlay, ladder.css). `surface=\"clear\"` composes ON a
       base tier (`glass-floating glass-clear`), so its `::before`/`::after` are taken;
       a scrim pseudo would CLOBBER the specular or grain. The mandatory scrim is
       therefore the FIRST of two comma-separated `background` LAYERS — painted FRONT
       (the translucent glass fill) over BACK (the `--background` dim) within the box,
       so the dim sits between the backdrop-filtered backdrop and the content (a child
       paints above both). The fill stays translucent so the backdrop still reads
       THROUGH the dim — see-through AND legible, one element, no pseudo collision. */
    [data-surface=\"clear\"],
    .glass-clear {
        /* The dynamic scrim strength — DERIVED from the sampled backdrop luma
           (dims MORE over a bright grid). `0.5` is the safe mid-scrim fallback. */
        --glass-clear-scrim-strength: calc(
            var(--glass-backdrop-luma, 0.5) * 40%
        );
        background:
            color-mix(
                in oklab,
                var(--glass-bg-clear),
                var(--glass-tint-source) var(--glass-tint-strength)
            ),
            color-mix(
                in srgb,
                var(--background) var(--glass-clear-scrim-strength),
                transparent
            );
        backdrop-filter: var(--glass-blur-floating);
        position: relative;
    }

}

/* glass/ladder.css
   The five-tier .glass-{wash,quiet,resting,floating,overlay} surface ladder + the W55 adaptive-legibility @container style(--glass-backdrop: light) bucket (co-located with its rung) + the .glass-opaque escape + the content-aware under-shadow.
   Carved from the glass.css cascade root; @import-ed by glass.css in cascade
   order, each in its own @layer components (isomorphic to the monolith).
   See docs/precepts/design-idioms.md §cohesion-aware partials. */
@layer components {
    /* ── Five-tier surface ladder (v0.8.0; visual only, no geometry) ──
       Tier semantics, alpha-monotonic per R3-spec §A:
         wash      ~0.30α  + light blur      (was `subtle`)
         quiet     ~0.50α  + medium blur     (the missing rung)
         resting   ~0.65α  + canonical blur  (was `default`; the speedtest plate)
         floating  ~0.80α  + heavier blur    (was `elevated`)
         overlay   ~0.95α  + heaviest blur   (NEW; modal-over-modal) */

    /* The five-rung surface ladder declares the unprefixed
       `backdrop-filter` only IN SOURCE. Authoring both forms manually here
       caused Lightning CSS in Tailwind v4 to dedup-and-keep-prefixed,
       which modern Chromium then dropped from the CSSOM (the prefixed
       alias is parser-rejected post-Chrome 113). AY.W-A11Y-PERF O-2a — the
       build now OWNS the prefix: `vite.style-assets.ts publishStyleAssets()`
       injects the `-webkit-backdrop-filter:` pair into the SHIPPED
       `dist/styles/*.css` as a LAST pass (the source stays unprefixed, so
       the Lightning dedup never sees both forms; the build emits the pair
       after the cascade is assembled). This GUARANTEES the prefix reaches
       dist/ regardless of the consumer's autoprefixer/browserslist — a
       Safari ≤17 engine (webkit-form-only) paints the blur instead of
       silently dropping the unprefixed rule. Verified by
       `proof:webkit-backdrop` (count-parity over the shipped dist). */
    /* AW.W23 — every rung's background composes the adaptive-tint recipe
       (`color-mix(in oklab, <rung bg>, var(--glass-tint-source) var(--glass-tint-
       strength))`). With the default `--glass-tint-strength: 0%` the mix is a
       no-op = byte-identical to the un-tinted rung; a consumer biases the surface
       toward a sampled backdrop hue by setting `--glass-tint-source` + a
       strength ≤30%. oklab is the mwg-preferred tint space for the TINT mix here
       (NEVER an `oklch(from …)` lightness-shift in the GLASS-TINT mix). The
       `oklch(from var(--foreground) …)` relative-color recipe DID land its first
       live consumer at BB.W-DARK-INK-WARM — the dark `--surface-tint-*` arm
       (`tokens/dark-arm.css`), where the tolerant α-mix absorbs the small high-L
       chroma (the gamut-map caveat documented + pinned under the gamut edge there).
       That is the INK derivation, separate from THIS glass-tint mix space. */
    .glass-wash {
        position: relative;
        background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--glass-bg-wash), var(--glass-tint-source) var(--glass-tint-strength));
        backdrop-filter: var(--glass-blur-wash);
        /* BB.W-GLASS-ACCENT — the rung border ink feeds the accent-composed
           `--glass-border-accent` (rim group above); byte-identical at 0%. */
        --glass-border-rung: var(--glass-border-wash);
        border: 1px solid var(--glass-border-accent);
        box-shadow: var(--glass-material-rim), var(--glass-shadow-wash);
    }

    /* AL-W10 SLIM under-shadow wiring (tokens.css §AL-W10 SLIM —
       UNDER-SHADOW recipe rungs). Each tier composes its canonical
       iOS Liquid Glass under-shadow (a 0.5px dark hairline along the
       bottom edge reading as glass thickness) as a NON-INSET layer
       PREPENDED to the existing `--glass-shadow-*` recipe — the under-
       shadow lays the floor; the existing shadow stack lays the lift.
         quiet   → `--glass-under-shadow-quiet`   (0 2px 8px -1px α0.04)
         resting → `--glass-under-shadow-default` (0 4px 16px -2px α0.08)
         floating→ `--glass-under-shadow-vivid`   (0 8px 32px -4px α0.12)
       `.glass-wash` is sub-perceptual; `.glass-overlay` is modal-heavy
       — neither composes an under-shadow rung (Y4/Y6/Y7 SLIM retention).
       Spine tier wires its own rung at instrument-chassis.css. */
    .glass-quiet {
        position: relative;
        background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--glass-bg-quiet), var(--glass-tint-source) var(--glass-tint-strength));
        backdrop-filter: var(--glass-blur-quiet);
        --glass-border-rung: var(--glass-border-quiet);
        border: 1px solid var(--glass-border-accent);
        box-shadow:
            var(--glass-material-rim),
            var(--glass-under-shadow-quiet),
            var(--glass-shadow-quiet);
    }

    .glass-resting {
        position: relative;
        background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--glass-bg-resting), var(--glass-tint-source) var(--glass-tint-strength));
        backdrop-filter: var(--glass-blur-resting);
        --glass-border-rung: var(--glass-border-resting);
        border: 1px solid var(--glass-border-accent);
        box-shadow:
            var(--glass-material-rim),
            var(--glass-under-shadow-default),
            var(--glass-shadow-resting);
    }

    /* AV.W15 M3 / AW.W22 — the floating tier carries the full-perimeter rim (the
       catch-light that \"defines the silhouette\", WWDC25 sess. 219). The rim now
       comes from the unified `--glass-material-rim` (= `--glass-edge-light`) so
       it shares ONE source with the rest of the ladder; it rides INSIDE the
       box-shadow stack so it composes with the under-shadow floor + the lift. */
    .glass-floating {
        /* AY.W-A11Y-PERF O-1 — engage the W55 bright-backdrop bucket BY DEFAULT on
           the overlay band: a floating surface floats over WHATEVER the consumer
           painted (frequently a light/near-white app surface — the canonical G2
           case), so the library opts its own overlay-band glass in at its own root
           via the SELF-CONTAINER reach below (`.glass-floating { --glass-backdrop }`
           + `:where(.glass-floating) > * `-less: the rung re-points its OWN tint
           through the companion self-rule, not only the ancestor @container). `dark`
           (the :root default at tokens.css) stays the explicit per-surface override a
           dark-substrate consumer dials in on an ancestor. */
        --glass-backdrop: light;
        position: relative;
        background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--glass-bg-floating), var(--glass-tint-source) var(--glass-tint-strength));
        backdrop-filter: var(--glass-blur-floating);
        --glass-border-rung: var(--glass-border-floating);
        border: 1px solid var(--glass-border-accent);
        box-shadow:
            var(--glass-material-rim),
            var(--glass-under-shadow-vivid),
            var(--glass-shadow-floating);
    }

    .glass-overlay {
        /* AY.W-A11Y-PERF O-1 — the overlay band engages the W55 bucket by default
           (see .glass-floating). */
        --glass-backdrop: light;
        position: relative;
        background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--glass-bg-overlay), var(--glass-tint-source) var(--glass-tint-strength));
        backdrop-filter: var(--glass-blur-overlay);
        --glass-border-rung: var(--glass-border-overlay);
        border: 1px solid var(--glass-border-accent);
        box-shadow: var(--glass-material-rim), var(--glass-shadow-overlay);
    }

    /* ── Adaptive legibility: the bright-backdrop bucket (AX.W55 — the iOS-26/27
       \"locally darken the glass over light content\" move; G2) ──────────────────
       When a consumer sets `--glass-backdrop: light` on any ancestor (a near-white
       card surface, a pale aurora bleed, a bright data grid), the `@container
       style()` read engages here and LIFTS the tint toward ink: `--glass-tint-
       strength` climbs from the `0%` rest default to the bounded AA floor
       (`--glass-tint-strength-aa`, ≤24% — translucent floor preserved) and `--glass-
       tint-source` re-points from `var(--card)` to the warm-ink `--glass-tint-ink`.
       Both are INHERITING custom properties the rungs' `background:` composite
       already reads, so re-pointing them on the material group re-resolves the
       EXISTING `color-mix(in oklab, <rung bg>, …)` toward ink — ZERO new compositing
       seam, the literal darken-over-light recipe. The `.glass-*::before` specular
       `plus-lighter` gleam (W52) is untouched (it composes ON the darkened plate;
       the darkening is the orthogonal oklab-tint axis). No `@supports` wrapper:
       style queries introduce no new `container-type` value (every element is a
       style container by default), so an engine without style-query support parses
       the unknown at-rule as invalid, drops the block, and keeps the zero-delta
       glass (the graceful degradation — the surface is today's-glass, the pre-W55
       state; see utilities.css §density for the same precedent). */
    @container style(--glass-backdrop: light) {
        .glass-material,
        .glass-wash,
        .glass-quiet,
        .glass-resting,
        .glass-floating,
        .glass-overlay,
        .glass-card {
            --glass-tint-source: var(--glass-tint-ink);
            --glass-tint-strength: var(--glass-tint-strength-aa);
            /* BA.W-DARK-MATERIAL scope 7 — the --muted-foreground lift moves HERE, into the
               declared/sampled BRIGHT condition. The muted L40 tier cannot clear 4.5:1 on a
               translucent FULL-AA-darkened plate (darkening moves the plate toward muted's
               own luminance), so it lifts to the full ink ONLY where the plate actually
               darkens — NOT on the calm-light unconditional floor (where it stays the muted
               register, the slides caption read). */
            --muted-foreground: var(--foreground);
        }
    }

    /* AY.W-A11Y-PERF O-1 — SELF-ENGAGE the overlay band by default. The
       `@container style()` rule above queries an ANCESTOR (CSS style queries never
       self-match — verified on Chrome 148), so the `.glass-floating { --glass-backdrop:
       light }` default we set on the rung would NOT darken the rung's OWN surface, only
       its descendants. AND the `:root` library default is `--glass-backdrop: dark`
       (tokens.css), so a `@container style(--glass-backdrop: dark)` revert would ALWAYS
       fire (every element has the dark :root as an ancestor) — it cannot distinguish an
       explicit consumer `dark` from the default. So the overlay band self-engages
       UNCONDITIONALLY as the library default: a floating/overlay surface floats over
       WHATEVER the consumer painted (frequently a light/near-white app surface — the
       canonical G2 case the user reported), so darkening the translucent plate toward
       the warm-ink is the correct default. This re-points the tint tokens DIRECTLY on
       the overlay-band surfaces (`.glass-floating` / `.glass-overlay` — the Dialog/
       Sheet/Popover/DropdownMenu/HoverCard/Command/Tooltip/Toast/Select-content band),
       engaging the SAME oklab tint axis (zero new compositing seam). Over a DARK page
       the darken is sub-perceptual (warm-ink over an already-dark plate is a small
       shift); a dark-substrate consumer who wants the pristine plate sets
       `--glass-tint-strength: 0%` on the band or uses `.glass-opaque`. The in-srgb
       `--surface-tint-*` family is never touched (this edits only the in-oklab glass
       tint axis).

       AZ.W-ADAPTIVE-AUTO Arm 1 — the self-engage now EXTENDS to the plain CONTENT
       tiers (`.glass-card`/`.glass-resting`/`.glass-quiet`/`.glass-wash`), not just
       the overlay band. The W54 glass-first MAXIMAL default makes content-on-glass-
       over-a-bright-backdrop the common case, so every content tier self-darkens over
       light WITHOUT consumer opt-in — closing the C5-7 asymmetry (the content tiers
       painted near-invisible over light on every route). The prior W55 overlay-only
       scoping (which kept the default-path byte-identity canary green by testing
       `.glass-card`/`.glass-resting` with a bare no-ancestor div) is SUPERSEDED: the
       three now-self-engaged kinds are no longer byte-identical to the dark bucket
       (the canary narrows to the legitimately-zero-delta surfaces — the `.glass-opaque`
       escape + an explicit per-surface `--glass-backdrop: dark` override — and the new
       in-situ G1 π readback becomes the binding default-path truth). The muted body
       register lifts to the full warm-ink under the engaged plate: the muted L40 tier
       cannot clear 4.5:1 on a translucent darkened plate (darkening moves the plate
       TOWARD muted's own luminance), so the low-contrast muted register resolves to
       the legible dark ink (clears ~10:1) — a token re-point on the same inheriting
       axis as the tint. */
    /* The OVERLAY band (.glass-floating/.glass-overlay — Dialog/Sheet/Popover/DropdownMenu/
       HoverCard/Command/Tooltip/Toast/Select-content) keeps the FULL unconditional AA darken
       + the muted lift: it floats over WHATEVER the consumer painted (frequently a light/
       near-white app surface — the canonical G2 case), so darkening toward the warm-ink is
       the correct DEFAULT over an UNKNOWN surface. Over a DARK page this becomes the
       luminous-dark LIFT (the dark-arm re-tunes --glass-tint-strength-aa to 12% — the plate
       lifts off the deep page). A dark-substrate consumer who wants the pristine plate sets
       `--glass-tint-strength: 0%`. */
    :where(.glass-floating, .glass-overlay) {
        --glass-tint-source: var(--glass-tint-ink);
        /* BC.W-ADAPTIVE-RECONCILE (the fourier-analysis-M Tier-1 #1 fold, CONTINUOUS half)
           — the OVERLAY band SPECIFICALLY JOINS the continuous observer driver, the SAME
           clamp the content tiers get. The dropdown/Dialog/Popover/Select-content plate
           FLOORS to warm-cream when the measured backdrop is calm/dark, and darkens
           CONTINUOUSLY only under a measured bright backdrop — NOT the binary `@container`
           bucket jump, NOT a blanket unconditional darken (the retired 4.0.x 20% slab).
           The `BC.W-AUDIT` pre-fix already closed the UNCONDITIONAL-darken half (the band
           floored by default); THIS is the continuous earned-darken half. The MEASURED AA
           bar (the overlay plate clears 4.5:1 WHILE α < 0.86) is GLASS-LEGIBILITY-
           MEASURED's roster addition — the driver here, the bar there, no double-ownership. */
        --glass-tint-strength: clamp(
            var(--glass-tint-strength-floor),
            calc(
                var(--glass-tint-strength-floor) +
                (var(--glass-tint-strength-aa) - var(--glass-tint-strength-floor)) *
                max(0, (var(--glass-backdrop-luma, 0) - var(--glass-backdrop-luma-knee, 0.6)) / (1 - var(--glass-backdrop-luma-knee, 0.6)))
            ),
            var(--glass-tint-strength-aa)
        );
        /* BC.W-DIALOG-GLASS — the overlay band's muted caption keeps the on-glass MUTED
           register on a CALM modal (the SAME `--on-glass-muted` the content tiers read on
           their calm path — legible-AND-subordinate against the composited glass fill),
           NOT an UNCONDITIONAL lift to full `--foreground` that blackened the caption on a
           cream modal over a light page. The bright-bucket lift (@container --glass-
           backdrop:light below) moves `--muted-foreground` to the contrast ink in LOCKSTEP
           with `--foreground` only where the plate ACTUALLY darkens to AA — so a busy-
           backdrop modal goes full ink, a calm modal keeps the muted register. */
        --muted-foreground: var(--on-glass-muted);
        --muted-foreground-strong: var(--on-glass-muted-strong);
    }

    /* BA.W-DARK-MATERIAL scope 7 — the plain CONTENT tiers self-engage the SUB-PERCEPTUAL
       FLOOR unconditionally, NOT the full AA darken (the R9-1 slides gray-slab fix). Over a
       calm LIGHT page the floor (--glass-tint-strength-floor: 4%) is a silhouette whisper —
       the translucent cream card stays warm (L ≈ 0.94), no gray slab. Over a DARK page the
       floor re-resolves to the luminous-dark lift (12% in the dark arm — the plate lifts off
       the deep page, the desired register). The FULL AA darken engages on these tiers ONLY
       under the declared/sampled BRIGHT signal (the @container --glass-backdrop:light bucket
       above — where the muted lift also lives now). NO unconditional --muted-foreground lift
       here: on the calm-light floor the caption keeps the muted register (the slides read).
       A consumer opts a light-page card out via `--glass-tint-strength: 0%` on an ancestor.

       BB.W-ON-GLASS-FG (N13) — the THIRD-STATE on-glass FOREGROUND re-point lands HERE,
       BESIDE the BA seam (the SAME cascade context, the SAME content-tier set, NOT a new
       competing layer). The calm-light floor keeps the page-muted --neutral-5 at HEAD —
       but the muted ink is calibrated against the CANVAS, and on a TRANSLUCENT content
       plate (the glass-first common case) it composites toward the lifted backdrop and
       its contrast COLLAPSES (1.15-3.29:1 measured in dark). The re-point targets the
       muted register at the COMPOSITED glass fill: --muted-foreground → --on-glass-muted
       (+ the -strong twin), a register BETWEEN the page-muted and the bright-bucket full
       ink (legible-AND-subordinate; the caption stays quieter than body --foreground).
       This is NOT a fork of --glass-tint-* — it READS the seam context and expresses the
       third state; it NEVER re-declares --glass-tint-ink/-strength-* (W-DARK-MATERIAL's
       frozen bound). The BA bright-bucket lift (@container --glass-backdrop:light above,
       --muted-foreground → --foreground) STILL WINS where the plate darkens — the
       @container conditional overrides this unconditional :where() when it engages, so a
       darkened plate goes full ink; this calm re-point is the calm-plate case ONLY. The
       overlay band (:where(.glass-floating, .glass-overlay) above) is a DISJOINT selector
       set (it keeps the full-ink lift), so neither path collides. The values auto-flip per
       mode (the on-glass rungs carry their own light/dark per-mode arms in tokens). */
    :where(.glass-card, .glass-resting, .glass-quiet, .glass-wash) {
        --glass-tint-source: var(--glass-tint-ink);
        /* BC.W-ADAPTIVE-RECONCILE — the CONTINUOUS observer-driven darken (the dead-knob
           closed; req #7). The strength is no longer a flat floor: it is a clamp that
           READS the observer's measured `--glass-backdrop-luma` and lerps from the calm
           warm-cream floor toward the ≤24% AA ceiling as the backdrop brightens past the
           `--glass-backdrop-luma-knee` (the iOS-27 dynamic-range shift, apple-ios27.md
           §1.5). Over a calm/dark backdrop (luma ≤ knee, the `max(0, …)` ramp → 0) the
           clamp resolves EXACTLY `--glass-tint-strength-floor` (the GLASS-IDENTITY calm
           identity, byte-equal); rising luma ramps the lift CONTINUOUSLY; the clamp floors
           at the 4% identity and ceilings at the bounded AA — never an unconditional 20%
           slab (the AZ grey-slab is the OPPOSITE of this earned, measured track). This
           RETIRES the discrete `@container --glass-backdrop: light` bucket as the strength
           driver — the continuous calc subsumes it (the bucket survives only as a no-
           `@property` degrade fallback). */
        --glass-tint-strength: clamp(
            var(--glass-tint-strength-floor),
            calc(
                var(--glass-tint-strength-floor) +
                (var(--glass-tint-strength-aa) - var(--glass-tint-strength-floor)) *
                max(0, (var(--glass-backdrop-luma, 0) - var(--glass-backdrop-luma-knee, 0.6)) / (1 - var(--glass-backdrop-luma-knee, 0.6)))
            ),
            var(--glass-tint-strength-aa)
        );
        --muted-foreground: var(--on-glass-muted);
        --muted-foreground-strong: var(--on-glass-muted-strong);
    }

    /* AX.W55 — the `contrast-color()` foreground-ink flip (Chrome 147+/Safari 26+).
       A PROGRESSIVE-ENHANCEMENT layer, NEVER the load-bearing floor (Open-Q #5
       ratified): the declarative bucket above already carries AA on ALL engines (it
       darkens the translucent plate so dark text clears ~11:1 over white at the 18%
       floor — the live π readback ratifies). On a supporting engine `contrast-color()`
       REFINES the foreground-ink pick natively, anchored on the LIGHT BACKDROP signal
       (`--card`, the warm-white surface base the bright bucket implies) — NOT on the
       ink. `contrast-color(var(--card))` over the light backdrop resolves to the
       maximally-legible DARK ink (anchoring on the dark ink instead would invert to
       white, illegible on the still-light translucent plate). The bucket is the floor;
       this is the native refinement. Scoped to the bright bucket. */
    @supports (color: contrast-color(white)) {
        /* The bright-bucket ink flip — the content tiers + overlay band over a DECLARED/
           sampled bright backdrop. BA.W-DARK-MATERIAL scope 6 — the SYSTEMIC contrast-color()
           selection-inversion fix (approach A, lockstep-lift). At HEAD this block lifted only
           --muted-foreground (and the surface `color`) to contrast-color(--card) (white over
           a light plate, L100), but the ACTIVE control register (a SegmentedTabs active tab,
           the curve-picker selected label) reads var(--foreground) DIRECTLY (L90, un-lifted) —
           a control sets its OWN `color: var(--foreground)` which wins over the inherited
           surface `color`, so the inactive (muted→white) read BRIGHTER than the active
           (--foreground), inverting selection. The fix lifts --foreground ITSELF in lockstep
           with --muted-foreground: now an active register reading --foreground resolves the
           SAME anchored contrast-color ink as the lifted inactive, so selected ≥ unselected
           everywhere (the inversion gone for ANY --muted-foreground/--foreground selection
           pairing inside a glass card — the SYSTEMIC library-seam fix, not a curve-picker
           one-off). Progressive enhancement only — the declarative darken is the AA floor. */
        @container style(--glass-backdrop: light) {
            .glass-material,
            .glass-wash,
            .glass-quiet,
            .glass-resting,
            .glass-floating,
            .glass-overlay,
            .glass-card {
                color: contrast-color(var(--card));
                --foreground: contrast-color(var(--card));
                --muted-foreground: contrast-color(var(--card));
            }
        }
        /* BC.W-DIALOG-GLASS / BC.W-ADAPTIVE-RECONCILE (overlay-band arm) — the overlay
           band's `contrast-color()` ink flip is now GATED in the bright bucket above
           (the @container --glass-backdrop:light block already lists `.glass-floating,
           .glass-overlay` alongside the content tiers), NOT here as an UNCONDITIONAL
           flip. The retired unconditional `:where(.glass-floating, .glass-overlay)`
           rule blackened the ink even on a CALM modal over a light page — a translucent
           cream plate forced to pure-black `contrast-color(--card)`, harsh on the cream,
           and `--muted-foreground` flipped to the same so captions lost their muted
           register. Now: over a calm/dark backdrop the modal keeps the WARM-INK
           `--foreground` (legible, not pure-black) + the muted caption register; only a
           modal over a genuinely-bright/busy backdrop flips to the dark contrast ink
           (where the plate ACTUALLY darkens to bright-bucket AA), the `--foreground`/
           `--muted-foreground` lift moving in LOCKSTEP there (the scope-6 lockstep). The
           overlay band STILL self-darkens UNCONDITIONALLY (the tint-strength clamp above,
           it floats over unknown) — only the INK flips in the bright bucket. ONE
           reconciled flip, not two forks (ladder.css stays the single source). */
        /* BA.W-DARK-MATERIAL scope 7 — the content-tier ink flip is GATED on the bright
           signal (it moved INTO the @container bucket above), NOT unconditional. Over a calm
           LIGHT page the content tiers only carry the 4% silhouette floor (still a warm cream
           plate), so flipping the ink to the contrast-color DARK ink there is correct only
           where the plate ACTUALLY darkens to the bright-bucket AA — the unconditional flip
           would force a white-on-cream / dark-on-darkened mismatch on the calm card. The
           content tiers therefore take NO unconditional contrast-color flip; their flip lives
           in the @container bucket with the rest of the bright-condition darken. */
    }

    /* ── The opaque escape (AX.W54) ───────────────────
       The level-0 endpoint of the SAME glass machinery — a solid `--card` plate
       (the opacity seam → 1) with `blur(0)` (the blur seam → 0), routed through
       the ONE `--glass-level` knob. NOT a parallel solid `background:` recipe:
       a `.glass-*` surface that ALSO carries `.glass-opaque` keeps its glass edge,
       rim, and under-shadow (a solid plate with a glass edge, not a bare div), and
       the @supports fallback + the a11y brackets compose coherently. This is the
       explicit opt-out from the MAXIMAL glass default (dense data, forced-colors,
       a consumer that wants a flat surface). */
    .glass-opaque {
        --glass-level: 0;
        /* BA.W-DARK-MATERIAL scope 7 — the opaque escape suppresses the adaptive TINT axis
           too (not just the opacity/blur level). A solid opaque plate has no translucent
           backdrop to darken-over, so the self-engage tint is meaningless on it; zeroing the
           strength keeps the opaque escape bucket-INVARIANT (the unset floor and the bright
           bucket resolve the SAME solid plate — the byte-identity canary). Without this, the
           scope-7 unconditional content-tier floor (4%) tinted even the solid plate, breaking
           the opt-out's \"flat surface\" contract. */
        --glass-tint-strength: 0%;
    }

    /* ── Content-aware under-shadow modifier (AV.W15 M4) ──────────────
       Apple raises the under-shadow over text (so the glass reads as
       lifting OFF the text) and drops it over solid light. The modifier
       is ATTRIBUTE-DRIVEN, no JS: a consumer adds `.glass-over-text`
       (or `[data-over-content=\"text\"]`) to a text-bearing glass surface to
       swap it ONE under-shadow rung heavier, or `[data-over-content=\"solid\"]`
       to lighten it one rung over a solid-light backdrop. It reuses the
       existing `--glass-under-shadow-{quiet,default,vivid}` rungs (no new
       shadow value — `vivid` is sufficient over text), redefining only the
       leading under-shadow layer per tier; the lift stack is untouched.

       The selectors are per-tier so the swap is rung-correct
       (quiet→default, resting→vivid). `.glass-floating` already sits at the
       `vivid` ceiling over text, so the heavier variant is a no-op there
       (the rung saturates) — documented, not an error. */
    .glass-quiet.glass-over-text,
    .glass-quiet[data-over-content=\"text\"] {
        box-shadow: var(--glass-under-shadow-default), var(--glass-shadow-quiet);
    }
    .glass-resting.glass-over-text,
    .glass-resting[data-over-content=\"text\"] {
        box-shadow: var(--glass-under-shadow-vivid), var(--glass-shadow-resting);
    }

    /* The inverse — over a solid-light backdrop the under-shadow lightens
       one rung (the glass need not lift as hard off a flat bright field). */
    .glass-resting[data-over-content=\"solid\"] {
        box-shadow: var(--glass-under-shadow-quiet), var(--glass-shadow-resting);
    }
    .glass-floating[data-over-content=\"solid\"] {
        box-shadow:
            var(--glass-material-rim),
            var(--glass-under-shadow-default),
            var(--glass-shadow-floating);
    }

    /* Grain noise overlay — reuses paper texture SVG, no extra assets.
       BB.W-LIQUIDHOVER — the disco-grain pop (T8-F6) killed at the COMPOSITION CLASS:
       the `background-image` is ALWAYS PRESENT (decoded once at rest, NEVER toggled to
       `none`), and any STATE-driven grain engagement cross-fades the `opacity` ONLY on
       the thin `--glass-grain-engage-duration` (~120ms linear) clock. A `background-
       image: none → var(--paper-clean-texture)` engage decodes-and-appears in one frame
       (the pop); the opacity cross-fade is a compositor-cheap interpolation with the
       image constant — the only sanctioned grain engage. The `transition` lives HERE so
       a hover/active grain lift (wherever a glass surface raises grain on interaction)
       reads as a smooth fade by construction, not a discrete swap. */
    .glass-wash::after,
    .glass-quiet::after,
    .glass-resting::after,
    .glass-floating::after,
    .glass-overlay::after {
        content: '';
        position: absolute;
        inset: 0;
        border-radius: inherit;
        /* The grain texture is the `background-image` longhand (NOT the `background`
           shorthand) so the image stays a STABLE always-present layer the engage
           never re-declares — the structural anti-pop: opacity is the only animated
           channel, the image is never `none → image`. */
        background-image: var(--paper-clean-texture);
        background-repeat: repeat;
        background-size: var(--paper-texture-size);
        opacity: var(--glass-grain-opacity);
        /* The opacity cross-fade engage — plain ~120ms linear direct-write (no spring,
           no overshoot — instant-but-smooth per the ask). Under the PRM / reduced-
           transparency brackets the clock collapses to 0ms (the a11y bracket below). */
        transition: opacity var(--glass-grain-engage-duration, 120ms) linear;
        pointer-events: none;
        mix-blend-mode: overlay;
    }

    /* BB.W-LIQUIDHOVER — the grain engage collapses to INSTANT under reduced motion (a
       grain that cross-fades on hover under reduce is still motion; accessibility is
       absolute). The reduced-transparency / forced-colors brackets already zero
       `--glass-grain-opacity` (a11y-fallback.css), so the grain is inert there; this
       bracket covers the reduced-MOTION case where the grain still paints but must not
       animate. */
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        .glass-wash::after,
        .glass-quiet::after,
        .glass-resting::after,
        .glass-floating::after,
        .glass-overlay::after {
            transition: none;
        }
    }

    /* The unified rim (AW.W22) + the BB.W-GLASS-ACCENT per-INSTANCE chromatic-RIM
       tint group (the rim-ink + --glass-material-rim ring + the per-rung
       --glass-border-accent border, light + dark arms) lives in glass/rim.css
       (carved at BB.W-CARVE4 — the next adjacent @layer components block, the SOLE
       writer of --glass-material-rim/--glass-border-accent). */

    .dark .glass-wash::after,
    .dark .glass-quiet::after,
    .dark .glass-resting::after,
    .dark .glass-floating::after,
    .dark .glass-overlay::after {
        mix-blend-mode: soft-light;
    }

}

/* glass/accent-tone.css
   BC.W-ACCENT-TONE — the contrast-floored 3-channel tonal-accent register.

   ONE `--tone` (a complete `<color>`, default `var(--primary)`) → a coherent,
   contrast-SAFE four-channel tonal set:

     --accent-fill  idle FILL  — faint but FLOORED (≥3:1 at rest, the load-bearing
                                 new behavior fourier named: \"not just hover/active\").
     --accent-band  active BAND — the bolder selected fill.
     --accent-edge  active EDGE — the active rim hairline.
     --accent-ink   label INK  — the contrast-SAFE label colour ON the band, written
                                 by useAccentTone (the JS half) via value.js
                                 safeAccentColor; the CSS fallback is the warm ink.

   The mix space is `in oklab` (the perceptual glass-tint family, W55/W-NO-GRAY) —
   NOT `in srgb` (the `--surface-tint-*` brand-overlay fence, AW.W26). This is the
   CONTROL tonal-FILL register (the plate background + the contrast-safe ink), a
   DISTINCT seam from the BB `--glass-accent` glass-RIM axis (decoration on a glass
   plate) — the two COMPOSE on a chip that is both a glass surface and a tonal
   control (the rim from `--glass-accent`, the fill from `--accent-tone`); they
   never fork, never write each other's channels.

   The strength MAGNITUDES are TOKENS a `:root`/scope override retunes (NOT props —
   the no-over-prop fence, component-customizability §5): `--accent-fill-strength`
   (idle, the ~6-8% fourier names) / `--accent-band-strength` (active) /
   `--accent-edge-strength` (rim). The idle floor is the strength default — the
   8% over the warm-cream `--card` clears ≥3:1; proof:accent-tone A4 measures the
   resolved idle fill against the surface (a strength that drops it below 3:1 reds).

   @import-ed by glass.css in cascade order IMMEDIATELY AFTER ladder.css (the same
   @layer components; this is the SOLE writer of the `--accent-*` channels, read at
   use-time on the chip elements, so the placement is cascade-order-invariant). */
@layer components {
    .accent-tone {
        /* `--surface` is an OPTIONAL consumer-retarget hook (NOT a declared HEAD
           token) — a consumer sets it on the scope to point the accent at a
           non-`--card` surface; it defaults to the declared `--card` so the recipe
           resolves at HEAD with zero consumer wiring. */
        --accent-fill-strength: 8%;
        --accent-band-strength: 18%;
        --accent-edge-strength: 55%;

        /* idle: faint but FLOORED — the resting tint stays ≥3:1 by the strength
           floor + the perceptual `in oklab` mix (the glass-tint family). */
        --accent-fill: color-mix(
            in oklab,
            var(--surface, var(--card)),
            var(--tone, var(--primary)) var(--accent-fill-strength)
        );
        --accent-band: color-mix(
            in oklab,
            var(--surface, var(--card)),
            var(--tone, var(--primary)) var(--accent-band-strength)
        );
        --accent-edge: color-mix(
            in oklab,
            transparent,
            var(--tone, var(--primary)) var(--accent-edge-strength)
        );
        /* ink is the contrast-SAFE label colour ON the active band — written by
           useAccentTone (the JS half) via safeAccentColor onto
           `--accent-ink-resolved`; the CSS fallback is the warm ink (CSS cannot
           call value.js at the token tier). */
        --accent-ink: var(--accent-ink-resolved, var(--foreground));
    }
}

/* glass/rim.css
   The BB.W-GLASS-ACCENT per-INSTANCE chromatic rim/accent group (the rim-ink +
   --glass-material-rim ring + the per-rung --glass-border-accent border, light +
   dark arms).
   Carved from glass/ladder.css at BB.W-CARVE4 to hold the no-god-module bound (the
   five-rung surface ladder + the W55 bright-bucket @container + the calm-tier re-point
   + the contrast-color ink-flip + the opaque escape + the under-shadow modifier + the
   grain ::after stay in ladder.css). @import-ed by glass.css in cascade order
   IMMEDIATELY AFTER ladder.css — the SAME @layer components; the rim/accent group is
   the SOLE writer of --glass-material-rim/--glass-border-accent (the rungs read those
   + --glass-rim-ink as custom properties resolved at use-time on the SAME elements, so
   the relocation is cascade-order-invariant), and the W55 bright-bucket is DELIBERATELY
   kept in ladder.css (relocating it past the contrast-color @supports block would invert
   their same-specificity source-order tie on supporting engines — a behaviour change the
   byte-isomorphism bound forbids). The dist is byte-isomorphic. */
@layer components {
    /* (b) The unified rim — `--glass-edge-light` full-perimeter inset ring on
       EVERY material surface (AW.W22). Previously the rim was wired only into
       the floating/overlay tiers' box-shadow stack; promoting it to the material
       group makes wash/quiet/resting/card/dock-control all carry the same
       silhouette catch-light from ONE token. The rim is its OWN `box-shadow`
       declaration on the material group so it composes uniformly without
       perturbing each tier's elevation recipe (the floating/overlay tiers drop
       their now-redundant inline `--glass-edge-light` layer below). The alpha is
       deliberately low so the rim never eats AA legibility over a busy
       backdrop. */
    /* ── BB.W-GLASS-ACCENT — the per-INSTANCE chromatic-RIM tint ───────────────
       The THIRD disjoint glass axis (LEVEL · TINT · ACCENT). The rim is TWO
       resolved things — the `--glass-material-rim` inset RING (= `--glass-edge-
       light`, an inset box-shadow) and the per-rung `--glass-border-*` BORDER —
       and BOTH must carry the accent hue so the rim reads as ONE accent silhouette,
       not a half-tinted edge.

       The composition is `color-mix(in oklab, <current rim color>, var(--glass-
       accent) var(--glass-accent-strength))` — the PERCEPTUAL oklab mix the W55
       glass-tint seam + the `<IconChip>` chip already speak (NOT in-srgb — the
       in-srgb fence is the `--surface-tint-*` brand-overlay family, a SEPARATE
       axis this never touches). At the resting `--glass-accent-strength: 0%` (the
       `@property` default) + `--glass-accent: transparent` (the neutral identity)
       the mix resolves byte-identical to today's rim (the W2 no-op floor).

       The RING: a box-shadow value cannot be `color-mix`-ed whole, so the rim ring
       is REBUILT here as `inset 0 0 0 0.75px <mixed-color>` — the SAME 0.75px inset
       geometry the `--glass-edge-light` token paints (AV.W15 M3), only its COLOR
       stop composing the accent. At 0% strength the color resolves to the
       `--glass-edge-light` base ink (`hsl(0 0% 100% / 0.18)` light / 0.22 dark —
       read via `--glass-rim-ink`) so the ring is byte-identical to
       `var(--glass-edge-light)`. The `.dark` arm re-points `--glass-rim-ink` to the
       dark base so the dark rim composes off `--glass-edge-light-dark`.

       The BORDER: each rung's `border-color` reads `--glass-border-accent`, which
       composes the accent onto the rung's own `--glass-border-*` ink. The rung
       borders are re-pointed at their declarations (the `border: 1px solid
       var(--glass-border-accent)`) so ONE derived token carries the accent across
       the band; `--glass-border-accent` reads the per-rung `--glass-border-rung`
       the rung sets locally, mixed with the accent.

       This axis NEVER touches the plate BACKGROUND (the W55 `--glass-tint-source`
       cohort) — the accent is the rim+glint chromatic DECORATION, not the
       whole-plate legibility darken (the distinct-axis-not-fork fence). */
    .glass-material,
    .glass-wash,
    .glass-quiet,
    .glass-resting,
    .glass-floating,
    .glass-overlay,
    .glass-card,
    .glass-specular-track,
    .dock-icon-button {
        /* BC.W-BLACK-BAR D2 — the rim ring is now DIRECTIONAL: the top catch-light
           (`--glass-rim-top`, glass-fx.css) carries the white-α ink the old
           omnidirectional ring carried (0.30 light / 0.40 dark — the dark arm
           re-points it via dark-arm.css), and the bottom under-shadow
           (`--glass-rim-bottom`) grounds the plate. The accent (BB.W-GLASS-ACCENT)
           still mixes ONTO the top catch-light ink so the chromatic rim reads on the
           lit edge; at 0% strength the mix resolves byte-identical to the bare
           `--glass-rim-top`. `--glass-rim-ink` is the resolved top-ink stop (kept as
           the accent-compose seam — the `.dark` arm below re-points it). */
        --glass-rim-ink: hsl(0 0% 100% / 0.30);
        /* BD.W-GLASS-KEY-EDGE — the LIT catch is now KEY-DRIVEN: the accent-mixed
           catch-light rides the key-facing edges (`--glass-key-lit-y` top +
           `--glass-key-lit-x` side, the upper-right cel key) so the accent reads on
           the LIT edge that agrees with the cartoon cast's light source — not a flat
           top-only halo. The side stop carries the same accent-mixed ink at the rim's
           own 0.18-class weight (via `--glass-rim-ink`-derived mix). The SHADE half is
           the key-driven `--glass-rim-bottom` two-stop under-shadow. At 0% accent
           strength the mix resolves byte-compatible to the bare key-driven rim (the W2
           no-op floor). The moving-specular `::before` catch-light + the grain
           `::after` are UNTOUCHED (both occupied). */
        --glass-material-rim:
            inset 0 var(--glass-key-lit-y) 0
                color-mix(in oklab, var(--glass-rim-ink), var(--glass-accent) var(--glass-accent-strength)),
            inset var(--glass-key-lit-x) 0 0
                color-mix(in oklab, hsl(0 0% 100% / 0.18), var(--glass-accent) var(--glass-accent-strength)),
            var(--glass-rim-bottom);
        /* the per-rung border ink, accent-composed. `--glass-border-rung` is set by
           each rung at its own declaration (it reads the rung's `--glass-border-*`
           token); the accent mixes onto it so the BORDER half of the rim carries the
           same hue as the RING. At 0% strength === the un-mixed rung ink. */
        --glass-border-accent: color-mix(
            in oklab,
            var(--glass-border-rung, var(--glass-border)),
            var(--glass-accent) var(--glass-accent-strength)
        );
    }
    .dark .glass-material,
    .dark .glass-wash,
    .dark .glass-quiet,
    .dark .glass-resting,
    .dark .glass-floating,
    .dark .glass-overlay,
    .dark .glass-card,
    .dark .glass-specular-track,
    .dark .dock-icon-button {
        /* BC.W-BLACK-BAR D2 — the dark top catch-light ink is the lifted 0.40 stop
           (mirroring the dark `--glass-rim-top`, dark-arm.css) so the accent composes
           off the dark silhouette device (W-DARK-MATERIAL): dark glass needs a
           brighter rim to read over the deep canvas. */
        --glass-rim-ink: hsl(0 0% 100% / 0.40);
    }

}

/* glass/surfaces.css
   The convenience-shorthand surfaces — .glass-card / .glass-pill / .glass-btn + the glass pill-input + the chromatic edge-dispersion rim fringe.
   Carved from the glass.css cascade root; @import-ed by glass.css in cascade
   order, each in its own @layer components (isomorphic to the monolith).
   See docs/precepts/design-idioms.md §cohesion-aware partials. */
@layer components {
    /* ── Convenience shorthands (tier + shape) ── */

    /* Static surface utility — no hover lift, no transition.
       Interactive cards live in <Card> (which owns its own hover composition)
       or in components that explicitly opt into a hover variant. */
    .glass-card {
        /* Focus-elevation rungs (AQ.W3 §W3.1a) — identity = at-rest paint; the
           `:has(:focus-visible)` rule redefines them (one source, shared with
           the fallback). */
        --card-focus-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
        --card-focus-border: var(--glass-border-quiet);
        position: relative;
        background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--glass-bg-quiet), var(--glass-tint-source) var(--glass-tint-strength));
        backdrop-filter: var(--glass-blur-quiet);
        /* BB.W-GLASS-ACCENT — the card border feeds its focus-elevation ink into the
           accent-composed `--glass-border-accent` (ladder.css rim group); byte-
           identical at 0% strength. The focus rung still re-points `--card-focus-
           border`, which flows through `--glass-border-rung`. */
        --glass-border-rung: var(--card-focus-border);
        border: 1px solid var(--glass-border-accent);
        border-radius: var(--radius-card);
        box-shadow: var(--glass-material-rim), var(--card-focus-shadow);
        /* AY.W-A11Y-PERF O-4 — upgrade `layout style` → include `paint` so the
           maximal-glass nested backdrop-filter sample (a glass Button in this card)
           does not escape the card box (the standard nested-backdrop-cost mitigation;
           `contain: paint` clips DESCENDANTS only — the card's own rim/drop-shadow is
           NOT clipped, device-verified). */
        contain: layout style paint;
        transition:
            box-shadow var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard),
            border-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard);
    }

    /* Elevate one rung on descendant KEYBOARD focus (`:focus-visible`, not
       pointer). Scoped to `.glass-card`. `:has()` Baseline Widely; the
       `@supports not selector(:has(*))` class fallback (below) ships anyway
       (focus-elevation is UX-critical — child-state-styling MANDATORY clause). */
    .glass-card:has(:focus-visible) {
        --card-focus-shadow: var(--shadow-md);
        --card-focus-border: var(--glass-border-floating);
    }

    /* ── Glassmorphic circular icon button ── */

    /* BA.W-DEMO-AFFORDANCES (IG-A4) — the two size registers below are
       MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE; never stack `.glass-btn` and `.btn-pill` on one
       element. `.glass-btn` is the FIXED-square icon primitive (width/height:
       var(--size-icon-btn) + contain:paint) — `.btn-pill` is the CONTENT-WIDTH
       text pill. Stacked, the fixed square wins and contain:paint clips the
       wrapped label into a ~40px blob (the R8-17 \"play button\" defect). A
       text-bearing `.glass-btn` collapses the same way even without `.btn-pill`.
       For a play/replay affordance reach for <StoryPlayButton> (demo) or a real
       <Button> with a leading Lucide glyph, never an icon-button carrying text.
       Machine-locked by proof:demo-affordances (W1 — no class co-occurrence,
       no text-bearing icon button). */
    .glass-btn {
        @apply flex items-center justify-center cursor-pointer;

        /* Individual-transform identity base (AQ.W3 §W3.2) — `scale: 1` at rest
           mints the stacking context once so the hover/press scale below does
           not flicker it into being (the z-index/anchor hazard). */
        scale: 1;
        width: var(--size-icon-btn);
        height: var(--size-icon-btn);
        border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
        border: 1.5px solid var(--glass-border-wash);
        background: var(--glass-bg-wash);
        backdrop-filter: var(--glass-blur-wash);
        /* AY.W-A11Y-PERF O-4 — paint containment so the glass button's own
           backdrop-filter sample stays inside the box when it nests in a glass card /
           dialog / dock (the nested-backdrop-cost mitigation; clips descendants only,
           the button's own rim/shadow is unaffected — device-verified). */
        contain: paint;
        color: var(--muted-foreground);
        /* AX.W52 §6 doctrine — the surface legs ride the bezier `--ease-standard`;
           the `scale` leg is reconciled onto `--spring-smooth` (was `--spring-snappy`)
           so the icon-button hover settles on the SAME ONE register as `.btn-pill` /
           `.tap-squish` / `btn-interactive` (the gate asserts one scale register
           across all four). The press still reads crisp — `--spring-smooth` ζ=0.86
           settles fast without a visible bounce. */
        transition:
            background var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard),
            border-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard),
            color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard),
            scale var(--spring-smooth-duration) var(--spring-smooth);
    }

    .glass-btn:hover:not(:disabled) {
        background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--background) 85%, transparent);
        border-color: var(--surface-tint-22);
        color: var(--foreground);
        scale: var(--scale-hover);
    }

    .glass-btn:active:not(:disabled) {
        scale: var(--scale-press);
    }

    .glass-btn:focus-visible {
        outline: none;
        box-shadow: var(--focus-ring-shadow);
    }

    .glass-btn:disabled {
        opacity: 0.35;
        cursor: not-allowed;
        pointer-events: none;
    }

    .glass-btn.is-active,
    .glass-btn[aria-pressed=\"true\"] {
        background: var(--surface-tint-10);
        border-color: var(--surface-tint-25);
        color: var(--foreground);
    }

    /* ── Pill button base ─────────────────────────── */
    .btn-pill {
        @apply inline-flex items-center justify-center cursor-pointer font-medium border-none;

        /* AX.W52 §6 identity base — `scale: 1` at rest so the hover/press scale
           below animates the `scale` longhand (not `transform`) coherently and
           does not flicker the stacking context into being (AQ.W3 §W3.2). */
        scale: 1;
        /* AX.W51 D18 — the pill GEOMETRY rides the ONE `--ui-scale` comfort axis.
           gap / padding scale by the master scalar; the font reads the
           `--control-text` register (`calc(--type-small * --ui-scale)`, the same
           `text-sm` register the Button CVA paints), so a bare `.btn-pill` consumer
           and a `<Button>` agree on the control font, and ONE `--ui-scale` override
           grows the pill's height (via padding) + gap + font in lockstep. Desktop
           default is the identity (scale 1 → the prior proportion). */
        gap: calc(0.375rem * var(--ui-scale));
        border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
        padding: calc(0.5rem * var(--ui-scale)) calc(1rem * var(--ui-scale));
        font-size: var(--control-text);
        /* AX.W52 — the ONE coherent button transition (RED witness 5/6). The
           SURFACE legs (bg/border/box-shadow/color/opacity) ride the bezier
           `--ease-standard` (a colour cross-fade reads as a wobble on a spring);
           the TRANSFORM leg (`scale`) rides `--spring-smooth` (the sub-perceptual
           settle), so the hover lift + the surface cross-fade read as ONE glide.
           The DEAD `transform` leg is retired — the variants animate the `scale`
           longhand. CRITICAL: `.tap-squish` (composed alongside on the button
           base, utilities.css) ALSO lists this full coherent set, so neither
           class's `transition` shorthand can clobber the other down to a
           scale-only animation — the cascade bug that left every button
           transitioning ONLY `scale` (the live-readback RED, RED witness 5). The
           doctrine: surface-props→bezier, transform→spring (tokens.css §2). */
        transition:
            background-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard),
            border-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard),
            box-shadow var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard),
            color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard),
            opacity var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard),
            scale var(--spring-smooth-duration) var(--spring-smooth);
    }

    .btn-pill > * {
        @apply flex-shrink-0;
    }


    .btn-pill:disabled {
        opacity: var(--opacity-disabled);
        cursor: not-allowed;
        pointer-events: none;
    }

    /* ── Glass-button backdrop ─────────────────────────────────────────────
       AX.W52 — the `glass`/`glass-wash` Button variants compose `.glass-wash`,
       which resolves `--glass-blur-wash` = `blur(1px)` — a sub-perceptual
       diffusion authored for a small detail TILE, not a button the user reads
       as \"glass\" (the live readback found the glass variants at a negligible
       1px). A hero control wants a real glass blur. `.btn-glass` re-points the
       backdrop at `--glass-blur-btn` (the `quiet`-tier 10px radius + the gentle
       `saturate(1.05) brightness(1.02)` light-concentration), so the glass
       button variants actually read as liquid glass while the wash TILE tier
       keeps its 1px untouched. Declares the UNPREFIXED `backdrop-filter` ONLY —
       Lightning CSS emits the `-webkit-` companion per the single-source
       authorship discipline above (hand-authoring both forms makes Lightning
       dedup-and-keep-prefixed, which modern Chromium then drops). */
    .btn-glass {
        backdrop-filter: var(--glass-blur-btn);

        /* BB.W-BUTTON-GLASS (d) — the button-scoped DEPTH stack + the hover-specular
           bump. The glass button was the flattest wash rung (a bare backdrop-filter,
           no rim, no under-shadow) — it read as a tinted rectangle, not a lifted
           glass lozenge. The `--glass-btn-*` knobs compose ALREADY-MINTED tokens (the
           `--glass-edge-light` rim + the `quiet`/`resting`-tier `--glass-under-shadow-*`
           drop) so a consumer retints the whole button depth from ONE override; NO new
           compositing axis. The rim+under-shadow lifts the lozenge; the box-shadow is
           additive over the variant's own focus/press shadow legs (the `.btn-pill`
           transition carries box-shadow on the §6 bezier, so the depth cross-fades
           coherently). */
        --glass-btn-rim: var(--glass-edge-light);
        --glass-btn-under-shadow: var(--glass-under-shadow-quiet);
        --glass-btn-under-shadow-hover: var(--glass-under-shadow-default);
        box-shadow: var(--glass-btn-rim), var(--glass-btn-under-shadow);
        /* The button-scoped hover gleam bump (0.10 → ~0.14): a hovered glass button
           reads a notch brighter at the grazing edge than the global hover whisper.
           Token-scoped to `.btn-glass` (NOT the global `:root` cohort property-regs.css
           owns) so only the button lifts. The active/rest rungs ride the global cohort
           (the press whisper + the dormant rest). */
        --glass-specular-intensity-hover: var(--glass-specular-btn-hover);
    }

    /* BC.W-BUTTON-GLASS-IOS (move 2 / BG-IOS-2) — the hero CTA reaches DEEP glass.
       `.btn-glass` reads `--glass-blur-btn` (not `--glass-blur-floating`), so the bare
       `.glass-deep` axis (which re-points `--glass-blur-floating`) does not deepen a
       button. This arm re-points `--glass-blur-btn` onto the deep family — the SAME
       token-substitution model `.glass-deep` uses (glass/deep.css), composing the ONE
       deep axis, NOT a new blur recipe. So a `default`/`primary-audacious` hero CTA
       reads the apple.com-nav-grade thick refractive liquid glass (16px / saturate 1.5
       family, `--glass-blur-deep`), while the plain content tiers + the quieter
       `outline`/`secondary`/`accent` glass buttons stay calm at the floating-tier
       `--glass-blur-btn`. The deep BLUR is budget-FREE (the W-LENSING budget binds the
       `:liquid` SVG lens, not the deep-blur tier). The a11y `--glass-level:0` brackets
       reach it for free (the deep blur composes `--glass-level`, the opaque-escape path). */
    .btn-glass.glass-deep {
        --glass-blur-btn: var(--glass-blur-deep);
    }

    /* The depth deepens one rung on hover (the lifted-lozenge read) — additive over
       the variant's own hover surface legs. The variants paint the bg/border on
       hover; this adds the deeper drop. */
    .btn-glass:hover:not(:disabled, [data-disabled]) {
        box-shadow: var(--glass-btn-rim), var(--glass-btn-under-shadow-hover);
    }

    /* BC.W-BUTTON-GLASS-IOS (move 3 / BG-IOS-4) — the press is COUPLED (W-MOTION-CANON
       P3): the ONE `--glass-btn-press-t` 0..1 spring drive (Button.vue writes it off
       `useSpringPress`) feeds BOTH the squish (the `useLiquidFlex` reciprocal X/Y deform,
       Button.vue) AND a SPECULAR brightness leg here — the touch-ILLUMINATION (apple-
       ios27.md §2.5 \".interactive() → scale + bounce + shimmer + touch-illumination\").
       During the press the button's specular tracks the SPRING (not the binary `:active`
       toggle material.css sets): `--specular-intensity` LERPs from the hover gleam toward
       the active gleam as `--glass-btn-press-t` travels, so the gleam ILLUMINATES on the
       SAME spring scalar as the squish (the glass reads as DEFORMING-AND-BRIGHTENING with
       the spring's alive overshoot, not a uniform shrink). It reads the EXISTING
       `--glass-specular-intensity-{hover,active}` cohort (property-regs.css) — no new
       token, ONE drive two legs. Specificity ties material's `.glass-wash:active::before`
       (0,2,1) and wins by source-order-after (surfaces.css @import-s after material.css),
       so the button press follows the spring; the hover gleam + the no-JS `:active` floor
       are untouched. */
    .btn-glass:active::before {
        --specular-intensity: calc(
            var(--glass-specular-intensity-hover) +
            (var(--glass-specular-intensity-active) - var(--glass-specular-intensity-hover)) *
            var(--glass-btn-press-t)
        );
    }

    /* BB.W-BUTTON-GLASS (a) — the ELEMENT-LEVEL oklab-tinted hover/active fill pair
       (the substitution-vs-inheritance trap fixed on the button register). The glass
       button variants painted `hover:bg-(--glass-bg-resting) active:bg-(--glass-bg-
       floating)` — the RAW `:root` rung tokens, which carry NO `color-mix(in oklab, …,
       --glass-tint-source --glass-tint-strength)` wrapper. So the W55 adaptive darken
       + the `contrast-color()` ink flip + the content-tier floor (all of which the REST
       bg `.glass-wash` composes via the element-level oklab seam) DROPPED the moment the
       pointer landed — \"more glass would cost legibility\" exactly inverted from the
       iOS-27 ask. These two tokens wrap the raw rungs in the SAME element-level oklab
       seam `ladder.css` composes for the rest bg + `--glass-bg-dock` for the dock — ZERO
       new compositing axis, the literal darken-over-light recipe reached at the lit
       fill. ZERO-delta at the `--glass-tint-strength: 0%`/floor default (the mix toward
       the source is 0%); under the declared/sampled bright bucket the lit fill darkens
       to AA in lockstep with the rest bg. The `:root` raw `--glass-bg-*` tokens are
       UNTOUCHED (other surfaces compose them at the element correctly — the raw token +
       the element-level oklab wrapper is the correct ladder shape). */
    /* BC.W-TABS-IOS — the tinted-FLOATING seam lifted off the `.btn-glass`-only scope
       onto a SHARED `:where()` so the iOS-27 segmented-control active pill reads the
       SAME element-level oklab-tint floating fill the glass button reads (the
       substitution-over-redeclaration discipline: ONE tinted-floating source, NOT a
       tab-local fork). The pill's active plate now reaches the W55 darken-over-bright
       seam — over a bright field the pill darkens-to-legible, over a calm field it
       stays warm-cream/near-white (the iOS adaptive register reaching the pill).
       ZERO-delta at the `--glass-tint-strength: 0%`/floor default. */
    :where(.btn-glass, .segmented-indicator, .glass-capsule) {
        --glass-bg-floating-tinted: color-mix(
            in oklab,
            var(--glass-bg-floating),
            var(--glass-tint-source) var(--glass-tint-strength)
        );
    }
    .btn-glass {
        /* BC.W-BUTTON-GLASS-IOS (BG-IOS-6) — the `quiet` rung gains the SAME element-
           level oklab-tint wrapper so the de-shadcn `outline`/`secondary` reskin (the
           QUIETER glass register, prominence-by-tint) reaches the W55 darken on its lit
           fill too — ZERO new compositing axis, the SAME seam the resting/floating pair
           composes. The floating rung is minted on the shared `:where()` seam ABOVE
           (the tab pill reads it too); quiet + resting stay button-scoped (the
           hover/active fill register the segmented pill does not engage). */
        --glass-bg-quiet-tinted: color-mix(
            in oklab,
            var(--glass-bg-quiet),
            var(--glass-tint-source) var(--glass-tint-strength)
        );
        --glass-bg-resting-tinted: color-mix(
            in oklab,
            var(--glass-bg-resting),
            var(--glass-tint-source) var(--glass-tint-strength)
        );
    }

    /* BB.W-BUTTON-GLASS (e) — the optional refraction-edge opt-in. CONSUMES the
       EXISTING refraction axis (glass-refract.css, the `#glass-refract` SVG-filter
       lens, `@supports (backdrop-filter: url(#…))`-gated with the un-gated blur base
       as the off-Chromium floor) — NEVER a button-local lens fork. BB.W-LENSING landed
       the squircle bevel-profile + the typed `--glass-refract` axis + the `.glass-lens`
       opt-in class (renamed off `.glass-refract`, clean break); the button COMPOSES
       `.glass-lens`.

       A `<Button :liquid>` adds `.glass-lens` to the host; on a supporting engine the
       button reads the edge-bend over its blur base. The base recipe (the
       `@supports`-gated `.glass-lens` rule in glass-refract.css) already paints the
       displacement filter over `--glass-blur-resting`; this block adds NOTHING to the
       filter graph (it would fork the axis) — the button merely COMPOSES `.glass-lens`.

       The `:active` LENS-SWELL is the CHEAP coupled read, NOT a per-frame displacement-
       map re-rasterize (the §Triumvirate / R2 budget: `feDisplacementMap` is resize-
       expensive — only the `scale` attr animates without rebuilding the map, and even
       that re-rasterizes; the one-refractive-element-per-route budget forbids the per-
       frame cost). The swell is the press SQUISH (Button.vue, the `useLiquidFlex`
       reciprocal X/Y deform) + the coupled `--glass-specular-intensity-active` gleam
       lift on the SAME `--glass-btn-press-t` drive — the glass reads as DEFORMING under
       the press without re-rasterizing the lens map. Off-Chromium the whole arm degrades
       to the un-gated `.btn-glass` blur+tint base (the no-workaround floor). The
       `--glass-btn-press-t` typed drive (property-regs.css) is the ONE press scalar the
       squish + the gleam coupling both read. */
    .btn-glass.glass-lens {
        /* The press drive scalar (0 rest → 1 pressed) Button.vue writes off the spring;
           the resting default is 0 so an unwired refraction button is the static lens.
           The `:active` LENS-SWELL on this drive lives in material.css's `.glass-lens`
           block (`--glass-refract` bumps on press); this block only floors the drive. */
        --glass-btn-press-t: 0;
    }

    /* The .input-pill / .control-surface form-control REST register is carved into
       glass/control-surfaces.css (a cohesive sub-concern, the no-god-module re-drain)
       — @import-ed by glass.css IMMEDIATELY AFTER surfaces.css, same @layer components,
       same source order (byte-isomorphic). */

    /* ── BA.W-PAGER — the glass pager pill chassis (.glass-pager-ring) ──────────
       The ONE pager-CHASSIS ring register: a `--radius-pill` glass-floating pill
       (the DockRail-chip recipe — `--glass-bg-floating` + `--glass-blur-floating`
       + `--glass-edge-light`/`--glass-specular` + the soft drop + the `.dark`
       box-shadow arm). It encapsulates BOTH the `<PagerDots ring>` dot rail AND
       the `<CarouselPager>` counter (the counter re-registers OFF the opaque
       `bg-card` slab onto this — the R9/R10-5 dark-slab class dies in the same
       edit). NEVER an opaque `bg-card` plate: this is the house \"controls in a
       glass pill\" idiom, translucent (reads the substrate through it), already
       dark/light-safe, already specular-rimmed (≥2-consumer-proven on the dock
       rail-extend chips + the dock collapsed pill). The legibility floor over the
       carousel's image content rides the SAME rebuilt registers
       (W-DARK-MATERIAL/W-NO-GRAY) — no local opaque fallback. */
    .glass-pager-ring {
        display: inline-flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.375rem;
        padding-inline: 0.625rem;
        padding-block: 0.3125rem;
        border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
        /* BD.W-GOO-CAROUSEL-DECK — the element-level oklab TINT seam (NOT the raw
           pre-substituted `--glass-bg-floating` :root token — the gray-hole
           substitution-trap). At the `--glass-tint-strength: 0%` default this is the
           no-op floor (`color-mix(in oklab, X, src 0%) ≡ X` — byte-identical pager pixels
           over a flat page); over a declared/sampled BRIGHT carousel image
           (`@container style(--glass-backdrop: light)`) the pill darkens-toward-ink for a
           real silhouette (the W55 ≤24% clamp keeps it translucent); under `.dark` the
           W-DARK-MATERIAL tint arm LIFTS it toward luminous warm-dark, never a charcoal
           slab. `in oklab` is the glass-tint perceptual family (the in-srgb
           `--surface-tint-*` brand fence is untouched). The dock `--glass-bg-dock`
           self-re-point precedent applied to the pager chassis. */
        background: color-mix(
            in oklab,
            var(--glass-bg-floating),
            var(--glass-tint-source) var(--glass-tint-strength)
        );
        backdrop-filter: var(--glass-blur-floating);
        -webkit-backdrop-filter: var(--glass-blur-floating);
        box-shadow:
            var(--glass-edge-light),
            var(--glass-specular),
            0 2px 6px -2px color-mix(in srgb, var(--shadow-color) 22%, transparent);
