       spring). Each row below is tagged [SPATIAL] (spring) or [EFFECTS]
       (bezier) so the rule is legible, not implicit. The canon doc is the
       canonical home; this table is the SOURCE it re-states:

         | Transition kind                                  | Tier      | Easing                   |
         |--------------------------------------------------|-----------|--------------------------|
         | Surface props (bg, border, color, box-shadow,    | [EFFECTS] | `--ease-standard` (bezier|
         |   opacity)                                       |           |   — a colour cross-fade  |
         |                                                  |           |   reads as a wobble on a |
         |                                                  |           |   spring)                |
         | Transform — hover/press/active (scale, translate,| [SPATIAL] | `--spring-smooth` (the   |
         |   rotate)                                         |           |   ONE button/interactive |
         |                                                  |           |   scale register) or     |
         |                                                  |           |   `--spring-snappy`      |
         | Size / morph (dock V↔H, indicator size-          | [SPATIAL] | `--spring-dock` /        |
         |   correction, reveal source-rect bloom)          |           |   `--spring-snappy`,     |
         |                                                  |           |   expressed as a         |
         |                                                  |           |   COMPOSITOR transform   |
         |                                                  |           |   (P5) — NEVER an        |
         |                                                  |           |   animated width/height  |
         | Enter (mount, popover open, dialog in, reveal)   | [SPATIAL] | `--spring-bouncy` /      |
         |                                                  |           |   `--spring-snappy`      |
         | Bold decelerating arrival (the SOTA arrival —    | [SPATIAL] | `--ease-out-expo`        |
         |   reveal draw-on, big-distance settle)           |           |   (cubic-bezier(.16,1,   |
         |                                                  |           |   .3,1); the house expo) |
         | Exit (unmount, close)                            | [EFFECTS] | `--ease-out` /           |
         |                                                  |           |   `--ease-standard`      |
         |                                                  |           |   (bezier, NO overshoot —|
         |                                                  |           |   an exit must never     |
         |                                                  |           |   overshoot past gone)   |
         | Position-tracked (specular pointer follow)       | [EFFECTS] | `--ease-standard`        |

       The SOTA arrival ease is the EXISTING `--ease-out-expo` (alias →
       `--motion-ease-out-expo` = `cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)`, the house
       expo with the value.js `easeOutExpo` JS twin in the MOTION_CURVES table)
       — NOT a net-new token. The motion canon NAMES it as the arrival register;
       it is not re-minted (a duplicate alias would fail proof:animation-
       coherence's EASING-TABLE-BOUND arm — the curve already has its canonical
       + alias row in `curves.ts`).

       The BUTTON family (`.btn-pill` / `.tap-squish` / `.glass-btn` /
       `btn-interactive`) UNIFIES its `scale` transform leg onto ONE register —
       `--spring-smooth` — so hover + press read as one motion language (the
       hover lift is the restrained `--scale-hover-btn` = 1.05, not the 1.08
       `--scale-hover`). Springs read alive on TRANSFORM and ENTER (overshoot =
       physicality); a spring on a COLOR or an EXIT reads as a wobble/hesitation.
       `--ease-apple` is the ambient-only register (consumed solely by the Pulse
       aura `--animate-ambient-pulse-easing` — NOT a second smooth-ease authority
       for interactive transforms; those use `--spring-smooth`).
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ */
    --spring-smooth: linear(0, 0.09979 2.041%, 0.30180 4.082%, 0.51333 6.122%, 0.69190 8.163%, 0.82461 10.204%, 0.91382 12.245%, 0.96822 14.286%, 0.99774 16.327%, 1.01113 18.367%, 1.01507 20.408%, 1.01416 22.449%, 1.01127 24.490%, 1.00802 26.531%, 1.00518 28.571%, 1.00302 30.612%, 1.00154 32.653%, 1.00062 34.694%, 1.00010 36.735%, 0.99986 38.776%, 1.00000 40.816%, 1.00000 42.857%, 1.00000 44.898%, 1.00000 46.939%, 1.00000 48.980%, 1.00000 51.020%, 1.00000 53.061%, 1.00000 55.102%, 1.00000 57.143%, 1.00000 59.184%, 1.00000 61.224%, 1.00000 63.265%, 1.00000 65.306%, 1.00000 67.347%, 1.00000 69.388%, 1.00000 71.429%, 1.00000 73.469%, 1.00000 75.510%, 1.00000 77.551%, 1.00000 79.592%, 1.00000 81.633%, 1.00000 83.673%, 1.00000 85.714%, 1.00000 87.755%, 1.00000 89.796%, 1.00000 91.837%, 1.00000 93.878%, 1.00000 95.918%, 1.00000 97.959%, 1);
    --spring-snappy: linear(0, 0.10159 2.041%, 0.31119 4.082%, 0.53355 6.122%, 0.72176 8.163%, 0.86005 10.204%, 0.95002 12.245%, 1.00115 14.286%, 1.02488 16.327%, 1.03150 18.367%, 1.02891 20.408%, 1.02249 22.449%, 1.01542 24.490%, 1.00934 26.531%, 1.00480 28.571%, 1.00181 30.612%, 1.00009 32.653%, 0.99926 34.694%, 0.99901 36.735%, 0.99907 38.776%, 0.99927 40.816%, 0.99949 42.857%, 0.99969 44.898%, 0.99984 46.939%, 0.99993 48.980%, 0.99999 51.020%, 1.00000 53.061%, 1.00000 55.102%, 1.00000 57.143%, 1.00000 59.184%, 1.00000 61.224%, 1.00000 63.265%, 1.00000 65.306%, 1.00000 67.347%, 1.00000 69.388%, 1.00000 71.429%, 1.00000 73.469%, 1.00000 75.510%, 1.00000 77.551%, 1.00000 79.592%, 1.00000 81.633%, 1.00000 83.673%, 1.00000 85.714%, 1.00000 87.755%, 1.00000 89.796%, 1.00000 91.837%, 1.00000 93.878%, 1.00000 95.918%, 1.00000 97.959%, 1);
    --spring-bouncy: linear(0, 0.10599 2.041%, 0.33511 4.082%, 0.58672 6.122%, 0.80212 8.163%, 0.95655 10.204%, 1.04836 12.245%, 1.08867 14.286%, 1.09341 16.327%, 1.07802 18.367%, 1.05470 20.408%, 1.03159 22.449%, 1.01303 24.490%, 1.00049 26.531%, 0.99363 28.571%, 0.99114 30.612%, 0.99150 32.653%, 0.99333 34.694%, 0.99560 36.735%, 0.99767 38.776%, 0.99924 40.816%, 1.00023 42.857%, 1.00072 44.898%, 1.00000 46.939%, 1.00000 48.980%, 1.00000 51.020%, 1.00000 53.061%, 1.00000 55.102%, 1.00000 57.143%, 1.00000 59.184%, 1.00000 61.224%, 1.00000 63.265%, 1.00000 65.306%, 1.00000 67.347%, 1.00000 69.388%, 1.00000 71.429%, 1.00000 73.469%, 1.00000 75.510%, 1.00000 77.551%, 1.00000 79.592%, 1.00000 81.633%, 1.00000 83.673%, 1.00000 85.714%, 1.00000 87.755%, 1.00000 89.796%, 1.00000 91.837%, 1.00000 93.878%, 1.00000 95.918%, 1.00000 97.959%, 1);
    --spring-gentle: linear(0, 0.09415 2.041%, 0.27374 4.082%, 0.45506 6.122%, 0.60779 8.163%, 0.72570 10.204%, 0.81213 12.245%, 0.87336 14.286%, 0.91570 16.327%, 0.94445 18.367%, 0.96371 20.408%, 0.97645 22.449%, 0.98481 24.490%, 0.99025 26.531%, 0.99377 28.571%, 0.99604 30.612%, 0.99749 32.653%, 0.99841 34.694%, 0.99900 36.735%, 0.99937 38.776%, 0.99961 40.816%, 0.99975 42.857%, 0.99985 44.898%, 1.00000 46.939%, 1.00000 48.980%, 1.00000 51.020%, 1.00000 53.061%, 1.00000 55.102%, 1.00000 57.143%, 1.00000 59.184%, 1.00000 61.224%, 1.00000 63.265%, 1.00000 65.306%, 1.00000 67.347%, 1.00000 69.388%, 1.00000 71.429%, 1.00000 73.469%, 1.00000 75.510%, 1.00000 77.551%, 1.00000 79.592%, 1.00000 81.633%, 1.00000 83.673%, 1.00000 85.714%, 1.00000 87.755%, 1.00000 89.796%, 1.00000 91.837%, 1.00000 93.878%, 1.00000 95.918%, 1.00000 97.959%, 1);
    --spring-dock: linear(0, 0.10470 2.041%, 0.32797 4.082%, 0.57059 6.122%, 0.77747 8.163%, 0.92676 10.204%, 1.01801 12.245%, 1.06194 14.286%, 1.07303 16.327%, 1.06501 18.367%, 1.04857 20.408%, 1.03088 22.449%, 1.01588 24.490%, 1.00510 26.531%, 0.99855 28.571%, 0.99542 30.612%, 0.99467 32.653%, 0.99528 34.694%, 0.99649 36.735%, 0.99778 38.776%, 0.99887 40.816%, 0.99965 42.857%, 1.00012 44.898%, 1.00034 46.939%, 1.00000 48.980%, 1.00000 51.020%, 1.00000 53.061%, 1.00000 55.102%, 1.00000 57.143%, 1.00000 59.184%, 1.00000 61.224%, 1.00000 63.265%, 1.00000 65.306%, 1.00000 67.347%, 1.00000 69.388%, 1.00000 71.429%, 1.00000 73.469%, 1.00000 75.510%, 1.00000 77.551%, 1.00000 79.592%, 1.00000 81.633%, 1.00000 83.673%, 1.00000 85.714%, 1.00000 87.755%, 1.00000 89.796%, 1.00000 91.837%, 1.00000 93.878%, 1.00000 95.918%, 1.00000 97.959%, 1);
    --spring-press: linear(0, 0.09979 2.041%, 0.30180 4.082%, 0.51333 6.122%, 0.69190 8.163%, 0.82461 10.204%, 0.91382 12.245%, 0.96822 14.286%, 0.99774 16.327%, 1.01113 18.367%, 1.01507 20.408%, 1.01416 22.449%, 1.01127 24.490%, 1.00802 26.531%, 1.00518 28.571%, 1.00302 30.612%, 1.00154 32.653%, 1.00062 34.694%, 1.00010 36.735%, 0.99986 38.776%, 0.99978 40.816%, 0.99978 42.857%, 0.99982 44.898%, 0.99987 46.939%, 0.99991 48.980%, 0.99995 51.020%, 0.99997 53.061%, 1.00000 55.102%, 1.00000 57.143%, 1.00000 59.184%, 1.00000 61.224%, 1.00000 63.265%, 1.00000 65.306%, 1.00000 67.347%, 1.00000 69.388%, 1.00000 71.429%, 1.00000 73.469%, 1.00000 75.510%, 1.00000 77.551%, 1.00000 79.592%, 1.00000 81.633%, 1.00000 83.673%, 1.00000 85.714%, 1.00000 87.755%, 1.00000 89.796%, 1.00000 91.837%, 1.00000 93.878%, 1.00000 95.918%, 1.00000 97.959%, 1);

    /* ── PER-SPRING DURATION CLOCK (BA.W-GLASS-CAL Unit 3) ──
       Generated from `scripts/regen-spring-tokens.mjs` — DO NOT hand-edit.
       Each `--spring-<name>-duration` is the spring's OWN 2%-band envelope-settle
       time `t_s = -ln(0.02) / (ζ·ωₙ)` (ωₙ = 2π/response), derived from the SAME
       (response, ζ) PRESETS table the `--spring-*` `linear()` curves above
       derive from — never a hand value.

       WHY: the `--spring-<name>` curve is NORMALIZED to 0..1 and discards the
       spring's settle time. Pairing it with a generic `--duration-*` clock
       (0.2/0.3/0.45s) re-timed EVERY spring to the same wall clock — snappy
       (response 0.35s) and smooth (0.5s) both ran 300ms, so the crisp snappy
       register dragged a dead sub-pixel tail (the R10-2 read: within-1px at
       ~109ms but the transition ran 300ms). These clocks re-time UNDER each
       register to its own settle WITHOUT touching the §6 register canon (which
       spring fits which job): a `transition` pairs `--spring-<name>` with the
       matching `--spring-<name>-duration`. */
    --spring-smooth-duration: 0.45s;
    --spring-snappy-duration: 0.4s;
    --spring-bouncy-duration: 0.62s;
    --spring-gentle-duration: 0.51s;
    --spring-dock-duration: 0.66s;
    --spring-press-duration: 0.16s;

    /* ── DECK SLIDE-TRANSITION CURVE (BC.W-DECK) ──
       The full-viewport keyboard-paged deck's slide transition rides the SAME
       `--spring-smooth` register the deck's `DECK_SPRING` samples (the calm-settle
       `.smooth` family; the (response, ζ) pair lives in deck/constants.ts) — an
       ALIAS, NOT a new spring family (the donor's own fence + the W-GLASS-CAL spring
       fence). `--spring-deck: var(--spring-smooth)` so a deck slide reads as a calm
       settle, not a bounce; the JS half (`installDeckSpring`) consumes keyframes.js
       `springTimingFunction(DECK_SPRING)` for the count-up easing. */
    --spring-deck: var(--spring-smooth);

    /* ── GOO-MORPH SLIDE-TRANSITION FLOW CURVES (BD.W-GOO-CAROUSEL-DECK) ──
       The per-consumer dwell-flow curves the ONE `useGooMorph` engine reads off its
       `tokenPrefix` (`--{prefix}-flow`/`-duration`/`-max-stretch`). These are NOT new
       spring presets — they are the SAME geometry-law DWELL shape the dot-pip worm
       (`--pager-worm-flow`, PagerDots.vue) ships: a curve that rises to ~mid by ~46% of
       the clock, DWELLS at the midpoint (the FAT metaball NECK held open across the gap),
       then contracts. The dwell is what makes the morph read as weighty liquid rather than
       a fast flicker (the W-GLASS-CAL spring fence: a normalized spring `linear()`
       front-loads — reaches ~0.5 by ~6% REGARDLESS of ζ — so the neck would collapse in
       ~150ms then sit dead; the dwell curve spreads `--goo-t`'s 0→1 across the REAL clock).

       --carousel-goo-flow: the drag register — the dwell shape with a gentle terminal
         overshoot (+1.5%, the snappy LAND); the plate necks across the gap then snaps. */
    --carousel-goo-flow: linear(
        0, 0.02206 4.167%, 0.07922 8.333%, 0.15799 12.5%, 0.24486 16.667%,
        0.32632 20.833%, 0.38889 25%, 0.41905 29.167%, 0.43667 33.333%,
        0.4575 37.5%, 0.47833 41.667%, 0.49917 45.833%, 0.52 50%, 0.54083 54.167%,
        0.56167 58.333%, 0.58373 62.5%, 0.62943 66.667%, 0.69424 70.833%,
        0.76859 75%, 0.84373 79.167%, 0.91191 83.333%, 0.96658 87.5%,
        1.0023 91.667%, 1.01466 95.833%, 1
    );
    --carousel-goo-duration: 0.95s; /* the card-plate clock — faster than the dot's 1.8s
       (a card-plate morph is bigger, reads heavy sooner) yet still slow enough to dwell. */
    --carousel-goo-max-stretch: 1.24; /* the plate swell — raised (1.16→1.24) for a more
       decisive welling neck across the gap (JUDGE-2 §3: the merge read SUBTLE). Still
       LOWER than the dot's 1.45 (a wide plate stretching 1.45 reads as taffy). */

    /* --deck-goo-flow: the calmer presentation register — the SAME dwell shape with NO
       terminal overshoot (a full-viewport slide must not bounce — the vestibular floor;
       the curve clamps at 1.0, no >1 stops). */
    --deck-goo-flow: linear(
        0, 0.02206 4.167%, 0.07922 8.333%, 0.15799 12.5%, 0.24486 16.667%,
        0.32632 20.833%, 0.38889 25%, 0.41905 29.167%, 0.43667 33.333%,
        0.4575 37.5%, 0.47833 41.667%, 0.49917 45.833%, 0.52 50%, 0.54083 54.167%,
        0.56167 58.333%, 0.58373 62.5%, 0.64 66.667%, 0.71 70.833%,
        0.78 75%, 0.85 79.167%, 0.91 83.333%, 0.96 87.5%,
        0.985 91.667%, 0.997 95.833%, 1
    );
    --deck-goo-duration: 1.1s; /* the full-viewport register — slow + dignified. */
    --deck-goo-max-stretch: 1.1; /* the gentlest swell — a big slide necks barely. */

    /* Core cubic-bezier (exits, fallbacks, non-spring uses) */
    --motion-ease-standard: cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1);
    --motion-ease-out: cubic-bezier(0, 0, 0.2, 1);
    --motion-ease-in: cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 1, 1);
    --motion-ease-out-expo: cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);

    --ease-standard: var(--motion-ease-standard);
    --ease-out: var(--motion-ease-out);
    --ease-in: var(--motion-ease-in);
    --ease-out-expo: var(--motion-ease-out-expo);

    /* Apple-inspired — micro-interactions, switches, dock hover */
    --motion-ease-apple: cubic-bezier(0.25, 0.1, 0.25, 1);

    --ease-apple: var(--motion-ease-apple);
    --ease-spring: var(--spring-snappy);
    --ease-decelerate: var(--ease-out);
    --ease-accelerate: var(--ease-in);

    /* BC.W-MOTION-PRESETS — the brand \"convergence-reveal\" / partial-sum settle.
       The Fourier partial sum converges ONTO its target with a decaying Gibbs
       ripple — a monotone critically-damped approach, NO sustained overshoot past
       terminal. That curve IS `gentle` (ζ=1.0, overshoot 0), so this is the ALIAS
       reuse: `--ease-convergence` resolves to `--spring-gentle` (no new
       SPRING_PRESETS row, no second curve). The settle clock is the per-spring
       `--spring-gentle-duration`. The N-term build-up is the `useStagger`
       orchestration ON this curve (a documented recipe, not a new primitive). */
    --ease-convergence: var(--spring-gentle);

    /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
       §2.W BD.W-MOTION-WEIGHT — the GOVERNING motion scalar (rest decl)

       The universal driver-motion governor (the @property reg lives in
       property-regs.css §18 beside --ui-scale). 0 = still, 1 = full cel. Rest
       1/φ ≈ 0.618: present, alive, never manic (§L4/§L6). DRIVER-scoped: a surface
       the finger/route moved INHERITS this; an OBSERVER surface (auto-carousel
       content, list reorder under scroll) pins it to 0 LOCALLY. It co-scales squash
       depth, overshoot share, anticipation pull-back, and cartoon-shadow travel
       together so they read as ONE proportioned deformation.

       The SQUISH CAP is derived SITE-LOCALLY at the consuming element (the JS
       getters in useDockOrientationMorph / useTabIndicator / useTabDragMorph /
       useLiquidPress read `--motion-weight` off their OWN element and return
       `1 + (cap_token − 1)·(weight + (1−weight)·flexVel)/0.618`), NEVER a `:root`
       calc token — an unregistered `:root` cap calc over var(--motion-weight) is
       substituted at :root (0.618) and inherits the FROZEN text, so a child's
       scoped weight→0 would never re-evaluate it (the spike's load-bearing
       correction). The cap reads the EXISTING `--*-max-stretch` token directly with
       a (weight/0.618) factor — zero new `--*-stretch-k` coefficient cohort. */
    --motion-weight: 0.618;

    /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
       §2.X BD.W-CARTOON-PUNCH — the third pole, a RAW shaped linear()

       A single damped spring is monotone-from-one-side — it CANNOT dip below origin
       then overshoot past it. iOS-27's loudest moments want ANTICIPATION (pull back
       before launch) AND EXAGGERATION (punch past the spring fence). So it ships as
       a hand-shaped linear() raw token: dip ~−4% BELOW origin (anticipation — a
       thing no damped spring can do), cross 1.0, punch to ~1.22 (deliberately past
       the ≤12% spring overshoot fence — which is WHY it is a register, not a
       spring), settle. Exactly ONE sign change before the peak + a monotone settle
       after (the shape-sanity fence).

       NOT generated by regen-spring-tokens.mjs (which emits only (response,ζ)
       springs from springPresets.ts), NOT a SPRING_PRESETS row, NOT a MOTION_CURVES
       entry (MotionCurveKind is the closed spring|bezier union; a shaped linear()
       with a negative leg is neither). A raw --ease-* property — zero engine
       extension, zero TS change. Drives TRANSFORM/opacity ONLY (compositor-safe,
       both engines — Baseline 17.2+, plain linear(), no -webkit prefix). The surface
       composing it carries `--motion-weight: 1` in the SAME recipe (the loud-register
       coupling — so the punch is never accidentally muted to ~+13.6% by inheriting
       the rest 0.618). PRM zeroes the weight → the amplitude collapses (the springs
       keep their own respectReducedMotion endpoint-snap). */
    --ease-cartoon-punch: linear(
        0, -0.012, -0.038 12%, -0.018 18%,
        0.18 34%, 0.62 46%, 0.98 56%,
        1.14 64%, 1.22 70%,
        1.16 78%, 1.06 86%, 1.012 92%, 1
    );

    /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
       §2.A AMBIENT MOTION — Pulse aura tokens

       Breathing rhythm for `<Pulse variant=\"aura\">`. The 6s duration is the
       canon ambient-pulse rate; scale 1.0 ↔ 1.15 reads as a quiet halo at
       intensity=\"normal\" (Pulse remaps min/max from its `intensity` prop).
       Reduced motion: Pulse.vue parks at the min stop, breath cycle disabled.

       AX.W57 (P6) — the aura re-baselined from a glowing DISC to an AMBIENT
       halo. The egregiousness was doubly-stacked: a 0.55-density currentColor
       radial multiplied by an element opacity the `ambient-pulse` keyframe
       drove up to 0.95 mid-breath (so a saturated viz hue read as a near-
       opaque throbbing blob). The fix is two decoupled, single-source knobs:
         · `--pulse-aura-strength` (0..1) — the ONE gradient-density scalar
           both color-mix stops derive via calc() (replaces the
           `--pulse-aura-opacity-pct` / `-pct-num` twin desync trap — the
           percent and the raw number had to be hand-kept in sync).
         · `--pulse-aura-breath-{min,max}` — the aura's OWN element-opacity
           range, decoupled from the shared `--pulse-aura-opacity-*` shimmer
           tokens (those stay 0.55/0.95 for the neutral skeleton-breath, where
           a grey shimmer at that range is the correct soft swell).
       The loud old read is the explicit `intensity=\"vivid\"` opt-in. */
    --animate-ambient-pulse-duration: 6s;
    --animate-ambient-pulse-scale-min: 1.0;
    --animate-ambient-pulse-scale-max: 1.15;
    --animate-ambient-pulse-easing: var(--ease-apple);

    /* Aura gradient density — ONE scalar (0..1). The center stop paints at
       `strength`, the mid stop at `strength * 0.35` (both via calc() in
       Pulse.vue). 0.22 reads as an ambient wash, not a glowing disc. */
    --pulse-aura-strength: 0.22;

    /* Aura element-opacity breath range (the `ambient-pulse` keyframe stops).
       Quiet: the halo stays a wash at every phase of the 6s cycle. */
    --pulse-aura-breath-min: 0.28;
    --pulse-aura-breath-max: 0.42;

    /* Skeleton-breath shimmer range — the SHARED neutral-shimmer stops
       (`.skeleton-breath` reads these; a grey shimmer at 0.55↔0.95 is the
       correct soft swell, distinct from the aura's calmer breath above). */
    --pulse-aura-opacity-min: 0.55;
    --pulse-aura-opacity-max: 0.95;

    /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
       §2.B SECTIONED PROGRESS — phase-bus rail (AB.W3.T2)

       The sectioned `<Progress variant=\"sectioned\">` exposes one
       height knob + one track tint knob. Defaults size the rail at
       0.875rem (14px) — the W3 spec minimum (≥ 14px mobile, ≥ 18px
       desktop). Consumers override the height per breakpoint via the
       same token name on the consumer-side surface.

       The bar reads as a recessed glass channel: inner top
       catch-light + lower inner shadow + small outer drop. The
       depth lives entirely in the Progress.vue scoped block; this
       token only parameterises the rail BACKGROUND and HEIGHT —
       depth shadows compose from the existing --shadow-color rung.
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ */
    --progress-sectioned-height: 0.875rem;
    --progress-sectioned-track: var(--secondary);

    /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
       §2.C ANIMATION EXPRESSIVENESS

       Two idiom families (the audacious press-ripple RETIRED at BA.W-GLASS-CAL):
       • Progress lifecycle — the progress bar rides an intake pulse on
         rising-edge, a crescendo brightening at ≥85%, and a one-shot discharge
         flash at 100% (the crescendo is the typed `@property --progress-crescendo`).
       • Chassis phase-tint companion — `@property --phase-tint-amount` so the
         chassis backdrop warms toward the active phase, interpolating with
         `--phase-color` across the consumer's phase-handoff window (600ms default).
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ */

    /* Progress lifecycle — typed crescendo + segment-local durations */
    --motion-duration-progress-intake: 220ms;       /* rising-edge pulse */
    --motion-duration-progress-crescendo: 240ms;    /* leading-edge brighten */
    --motion-duration-progress-indeterminate: 4s;   /* indeterminate sweep period */

    /* Chassis depth-of-tint peak — the amount `--phase-tint-amount`
       interpolates toward when a phase is active. Subtle by intent;
       a 6% color-mix reads as \"warmth toward the phase\" without
       overwhelming the chassis chrome's resting register. */
    --phase-tint-peak: 6%;

    /* BA.W-GLASS-CAL (H2a) — the `btn-audacious` press-ripple knobs
       (`--ripple-radius-max` 12rem, `--motion-duration-ripple` 340ms) DELETED
       with the disco recipe retirement: the ripple was btn-audacious-private and
       has no surviving consumer (the calm CTA register's press is the §6
       `--scale-press-btn` scale beat). The `@property --ripple-radius`
       registration retires with it (property-regs.css). */

    /* Skeleton breath variant — the M.3 \"known-imminent\" loading
       register. 6s cycle (the canon ambient rate) so a known-imminent
       skeleton breathes in lockstep with the dial and the Pulse aura.
       Coexists with the 1.5s shimmer (sliding gradient) and the
       Tailwind animate-pulse (2s, short-wait) variants — distinct
       temporal registers per use-case. */
    --skeleton-breath-duration: 6s;

    /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
       §3  Z-INDEX SCALE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ */
    /* V.W2 T12 — Aurora background tier (sits behind --z-background).
       Consumed by `<Aurora>` in speedtest's App.vue (`-z-10` literal today). */
    --z-behind: -10;
    --z-background: 0;
    --z-content: 10;
    --z-controls: 20;
    --z-bar: 30;
    --z-header: 35;
    --z-dock: 40;
    --z-panel: 45;
    --z-overlay: 50;
    --z-hovercard: 120;
    --z-tooltip: 120;
    --z-popover: 130;
    --z-modal: 140;
    --z-fullscreen: 150;
    --z-toast: 160;
    --z-toggle: 999;
    --z-max: 9999;
    --z-debug: 99999;

}

/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
   BD.W-MOTION-WEIGHT — the driver / observer carve (design.md §L4, gate C8)

   The governing weight rests at the 1/φ centroid. LOUD DRIVERS — the dock morph
   stage, an earned celebration — pin it to 1 so even a SLOW user-driven morph
   rides the full squish/overshoot register (iOS reserves the weighty bounce for
   what the user drives). CALM OBSERVERS — a programmatic reorder, an auto-advancing
   carousel — pin it to 0: auto-motion that springs reads cheap. A single
   element-scoped assignment, the same seam the PRM carve uses, inheriting down. */
.liquid-stage {
    --motion-weight: 1;
}
:where([data-reorder], [data-autoplay]) {
    --motion-weight: 0;
}

/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
   BD.W-MOTION-WEIGHT / BD.W-CARTOON-PUNCH — the PRM carve

   ONE assignment zeroes ALL of the goo that rides on top of the springs. Because
   every squish/punch/shadow-travel reads through `--motion-weight` (the site-local
   cap collapses to `1 + (cap−1)·0 == 1.0`, the inert-cast travel/spread collapse to
   their rest, the §2c velocity boost is gated by the same weight), this single line
   zeroes the squash, the overshoot share, the anticipation pull, the cartoon-shadow
   travel, AND the velocity boost together. The springs keep their own
   `respectReducedMotion` endpoint-snap in SpringProgress; the weight zeroes only the
   GOO. The fade survives. `--ease-cartoon-punch` re-aliases to the non-overshooting
   standard ease (belt-and-suspenders — the weight=0 already kills the punch
   amplitude on a weight-scaled surface; the alias also removes the curve shape for
   any consumer driving a non-amplitude leg). */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    :root {
        --motion-weight: 0;
        --ease-cartoon-punch: var(--ease-standard);
    }
}

/* tokens/color-radius.css
   §4 radius reference · §5 warm-cream/muted-black palette · §5c constellation · §6 section palette · §6b viz basis.
   Carved from the tokens.css cascade root; @import-ed by tokens.css in
   cascade order. Same :root cascade context as its siblings. */
:root {
    /* §4  BORDER RADIUS — authored ONCE in theme.css's leading plain @theme
       block (AV.W16 TW1). A plain @theme mints each `--radius-*` as a global
       :root var, so the `var(--radius-card)` recipe reads across this file
       resolve from that source. The prior §4 twin here doubled the override
       surface (the anti-pattern the @theme-inline migration kills) — excised;
       retune the scale at theme.css. */

    /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
       §5  COLOR PALETTE — Warm cream / muted black

       Neutral scale: perceptually-uniform L* steps at hue 48 (warm paper).
       Each pair visibly distinct at 20px viewing distance.
           0 → 1  : 3 L   (page → soft field)
           1 → 2  : 5 L   (soft field → chip / tab-inactive)
           2 → 3  : 8 L   (chip → hover / highlight)
           3 → 4  : 12 L  (highlight → subtle border)
           4 → 5  : 25 L  (border → muted text, contrast jump)
           5 → 6  : 10 L  (muted text → strong-muted text, secondary register)

       Semantic aliases re-point into the scale — no back-compat shims.

       BA.W-NO-GRAY — THE WARM-CHROMA FLOOR (R10-5 \"No gray.\"). The ladder was
       SPECIFIED warm (hue 48) but RESOLVED achromatic — at 6-10% sat across
       L40-95 the painted OKLab chroma landed C 0.0055-0.0155 (below the ~0.020
       perceptual gray floor) AND at hsl-48 the OKLab hue mapped to ~95° (a
       yellow-green), NOT the warm-amber the --foreground ink carries (hsl-24 →
       OKLab H≈56°). The fix is CHROMA-ONLY at constant L: warm the hue toward
       the foreground register (hsl 28-34 → OKLab H 62-72°, warm MATERIAL not a
       cast) AND lift the saturation off the gray floor. The L is unchanged (the
       contrast contract — AA re-ratified, ±0.01 of HEAD). The mid/low-L rungs
       clear the strong floor C≥0.020; the L90 chip is gamut-bound (it cannot
       carry 0.020 at L90 without a visible cast — the triumvirate \"tinted not
       warm\" trigger), so it lifts to a materially-warm C≈0.013 (~2.4× HEAD).
       Machine-locked by proof:no-gray + tests-visual/no-gray.spec.ts. */
    --neutral-0: hsl(40 30% 98%);   /* L 98 — page surface (warm-cream, KEEP-NEUTRAL surface) */
    --neutral-1: hsl(38 26% 95%);   /* L 95 — soft field bg */
    --neutral-2: hsl(34 28% 90%);   /* L 90 — chip / tab-inactive bg (warm, gamut-bound C≈0.013) */
    --neutral-3: hsl(33 30% 82%);   /* L 82 — hover / highlight bg (warm, C≈0.025) */
    --neutral-4: hsl(32 26% 70%);   /* L 70 — subtle border (warm, C≈0.036) */
    --neutral-5: hsl(30 22% 40%);   /* L 40 — muted text (warm, C≈0.043; WCAG AA: 5.21:1 vs page / 4.90:1 vs muted) */
    /* W7-α' (AK-FD1) — strong-muted text rung. One principled ladder step
       (10 L darker, the 5→6 cadence above) past muted text toward
       --foreground (L10). Sits at the 1/3 point of the L40→L10 muted→primary
       gap: legible secondary text that reads less-faint than muted but not as
       assertive as primary. By-COLOUR per the canon (a neutral step, not an
       alpha mute). BA.W-NO-GRAY — re-saturated onto the warm identity in
       lockstep with the ladder (hsl 28 24% — OKLab H 62°, C≈0.038, the warm
       register; L held at 30). WCAG AA: 7.88:1 vs L98 page / 7.43:1 vs L95
       muted (cf. neutral-5's 5.21:1) — comfortably above the 4.5:1 body floor. */
    --neutral-6: hsl(28 24% 30%);   /* L 30 — strong-muted text (secondary register, warm) */

    --background: var(--neutral-0);
    --foreground: hsl(24 10% 10%);
    /* BA.W-NO-GRAY L1 — the warm-bias glass PLATE. --card was `var(--neutral-0)`
       (the near-achromatic L98 page, OKLab C 0.0017) — so every glass plate
       (Card/Button/Toast/Popover/Dialog/Sheet, all --card-derived) composited a
       flat gray slab over a flat backdrop (the G1/G8 defect, the user's \"no gray
       ... for cards, buttons\"). Glass is TRANSMISSIVE — over a flat page there
       is nothing behind to modulate, so the plate is its own near-neutral cream.
       --card DECOUPLES from the page to a warm-cream value carrying a real OKLab
       chroma (hsl 36 48% 97% → OKLab C 0.0062 at warm H 75°, L kept ~98 so the
       page-card SURFACE read is byte-stable), so the plate reads WARM over ANY
       backdrop — the plate floor (composited C ≥ 0.0035, ~2× HEAD) the gamut
       allows a near-white translucent surface. The DARK --card is W-DARK-MATERIAL's
       lifted hsl(24 8% 16%) (NOT re-warmed here — the dark register is its work).
       Bounded: the cream family, never a tinted cast (the triumvirate bar). */
    --card: hsl(30 85% 96%);
    --card-foreground: var(--foreground);
    --popover: var(--card);
    --popover-foreground: var(--foreground);

    /* R0G-5 (AO.W4) — warm-neutral public-data panel surface. One warmth step
       off --card (hue 44 vs 48, +4 sat, −2 L) so a consumer's aurora-OFF
       /dashboard/* surfaces read with the admin canon's warm-neutral chrome
       instead of the colder bare card. Opaque surface (the --card family), not
       a --surface-tint-* overlay. Consumer applies on /dashboard/* only. */
    --surface-public-data-panel: hsl(44 16% 96%);

    --muted: var(--neutral-1);
    --muted-foreground: var(--neutral-5);
    /* W7-α' (AK-FD1) — one rung less-faint than --muted-foreground for
       secondary text that reads too-faint at the muted rung over resting
       glass (CardDescription). By-colour neutral step, not alpha. */
    --muted-foreground-strong: var(--neutral-6);
    --secondary: var(--neutral-2);
    --secondary-foreground: var(--foreground);
    --accent: var(--neutral-3);
    --accent-foreground: var(--foreground);

    --border: var(--neutral-4);
    --input: var(--neutral-4);

    --primary: hsl(24 10% 10%);
    --primary-foreground: var(--neutral-0);
    --destructive: hsl(0 72% 50%);
    --destructive-foreground: var(--neutral-0);
    --ring: hsl(24 10% 10%);
    --shadow: hsl(24 10% 10%);
    --shadow-color: var(--foreground);

    /* AQ.W2 §4 — native-control accent. `accent-color` is an inherited
       built-in <color>, so per the §2b resolve-late discipline it reads a
       dedicated UNREGISTERED token set per-mode (NOT light-dark()) and is
       re-specified inside `.dark` — otherwise the accent freezes to the
       light shade at the cascade root. Light: muted-black checkmarks /
       sliders on warm cream. Dark arm (`.dark`) reads `--ring` (mid-tone),
       not the near-white dark `--primary`, so a checkbox's white checkmark
       keeps contrast headroom (accent-color.md §5). */
    --accent-color: var(--primary);

    /* Border hairline at whisper alpha — the canonical rung for
       section-spine / panel-edge hairlines a consumer wants to read as an
       iOS-26 whisper rather than a hard rule. Consumers compose this named
       rung instead of hand-mixing `color-mix(... var(--border) ...)`
       per-site (token-first). 45% is the canonical whisper. Auto-dark via
       `--border` (which is `var(--neutral-4)`, light-dark()-resolved). */
    --border-soft: color-mix(in srgb, var(--border) 45%, transparent);

    /* BC.W-SEPARATOR-FIX — the warm separator hairline ink. A rule reads as
       WARM material, not a grey `--border` line (BA.W-NO-GRAY floor): the
       split-rule segments + un-labelled hairline both paint `--separator-ink`.
       It is a `color-mix` off `--foreground` (`light-dark()`-resolved, so it
       auto-darks warm in both modes — the warm-ink hairline, not `--neutral-4`
       grey). 22% is a visible-but-whisper rule (crisper than `--border-soft`'s
       45%-of-grey; this one is foreground-derived so it carries the warm hue
       directly). A consumer retints every separator from this single rung. */
    --separator-ink: color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 22%, transparent);

    /* AQ.W3 §W3.4 — tokenized scrollbar thumb + track. Minted in the color
       block so the `scrollbar-color` / `::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb` rungs read a
       single source. Auto-dark via `--muted-foreground` (light-dark()-resolved,
       AQ.W2) — the OS scrollbar chrome also honors `color-scheme: dark`, so the
       custom thumb tracks the dark surface without an explicit dark mirror. The
       thumb α (25%) matches the prior inline `.scrollbar-thin` value byte-for-
       byte; `transparent` track keeps the scroll-pane glass reading through. */
    --scrollbar-thumb: color-mix(in srgb, var(--muted-foreground) 25%, transparent);
    --scrollbar-track: transparent;

    /* Foreground-tint rungs — canonical handles for the 36+ raw
       `color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) N%, transparent)` sites surveyed
       in R5 axis-1. Each rung's α is named in its key (4 → 4%, 22 → 22%).
       Auto-dark via --foreground; no explicit dark mirrors needed. R5
       vocab.γ gap row 1. Consumers land in W2.B + W3.C. */
    --surface-tint-4:  color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground)  4%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-6:  color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground)  6%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-8:  color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground)  8%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-10: color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 10%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-12: color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 12%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-15: color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 15%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-18: color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 18%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-22: color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 22%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-25: color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 25%, transparent);
    /* Higher-α rungs added at L.W5 (absorbs K R4 — 4 P1 sites whose
       literal `color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) {35,40,70}%, transparent)`
       expressions bypassed the canonical token family). See `docs/tranches/L/
       audit/W5-A-doc-cohort-proof.md`. */
    --surface-tint-35: color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 35%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-40: color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 40%, transparent);
    --surface-tint-70: color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 70%, transparent);

    /* Muted-tinted surface rungs — canonical handles for the 9 raw
       `color-mix(in srgb, var(--muted) N%, transparent)` sites in
       FuzzySearch / SegmentedTabs / Slider scoped CSS. (AI.W5-δ — the
       ProgressiveSidebar consumer retired at the wave; the canonical token
       rung lives on for the remaining consumers.) Auto-dark via --muted.
       R5 vocab.γ axis-1 row. */
    --muted-soft:   color-mix(in srgb, var(--muted) 30%, transparent);
    --muted-medium: color-mix(in srgb, var(--muted) 50%, transparent);

    /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
       §5c CONSTELLATION — Canvas2D proximity-graph lattice (AX.W17)

       The H.W4 legibility intelligence promoted from the slides deck to
       LIBRARY identity: the node tones, the edge hairline color, the per-mode
       edge-alpha multipliers, and the field-yields-to-type translucency knob.
       Only `--constellation-accent` (the anomaly tint) stays a CONSUMER preset
       (slides aliases it to `--ncsu-red`); the library ships a neutral default.

       PLAIN-hsl LITERALS, per arm — NEVER `light-dark()` and NEVER a
       `var(--neutral-*)`/`var(--foreground)`/`var(--border)`/`var(--card)`
       alias. Canvas2D silently REJECTS a `light-dark()` value into
       `strokeStyle`/`fillStyle` (the W30 86.3%-red-splatter cardinal defect),
       and the rejection is identical whether the value is a literal
       `light-dark(…)` OR a `var()` that RESOLVES to one — so the constellation
       tokens are plain-hsl LEAVES, the `:root` vs `.dark` cascade carrying two
       literals. `proof:constellation-tokens` clause (c) machine-locks this
       (the no-`light-dark(`-substring NOR transitive-`var()` static assertion).
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ */
    /* Node tones — readable on the warm-cream ground. The two-tone split (the
       `dim` half rides `-node-dim`). Warm-grey ink, NOT a neutral-ladder alias. */
    --constellation-node:      hsl(36 8% 46%);
    --constellation-node-dim:  hsl(36 8% 60%);
    /* Edge hairline stroke — warm near-black on cream (the plain-hsl complement
       the slides deck reads to fix the `--foreground` light-dark() leak). */
    --constellation-line:      hsl(24 14% 14%);
    /* Anomaly tint — NEUTRAL library default (a desaturated warm accent). The
       consumer overrides this (slides → `--ncsu-red`); the library does NOT
       bake a brand hue. This is the single consumer-preset boundary. */
    --constellation-accent:    hsl(18 58% 46%);
    /* Edge-alpha multipliers — replace the magic `0.17` hairline / pointer-web
       literals. The cream ground lifts the node tones, so the field can recede;
       the focus-proximity edge reads a touch stronger. */
    --constellation-edge-alpha:          0.22;
    --constellation-edge-focus-alpha:  0.34;
    /* §7 field-yields-to-type translucency knob — the legible-but-RECESSIVE
       MIDPOINT, NOT max-legibility. On cream the node tones already read, so the
       field recedes further: BELOW the slides 0.92 max-legibility reference. */
    --constellation-alpha:               0.80;
    /* ACCENT-edge skin weights (AZ.W-CON-GEN G3) — the flagged-node EDGE legibility
       set the optional `drawEdges(…, accentIndex)` pass reads. `--constellation-accent`
       (above) is the edge TINT; `-edge-floor` is the neutral-edge alpha MINIMUM so a
       distance-faded hairline clears the perceptual floor on a bright ground (default
       0 → byte-identical, the consumer lifts it on a very-light backdrop);
       `-edge-accent-alpha` is the accent-incident edge multiplier (the flagged-node
       tether reads a touch louder than the neutral lattice). Mode-arm where they
       differ — the dark arm carries its own (dark-arm.css §5c). */
    --constellation-edge-floor:          0;
    --constellation-edge-accent-alpha:   0.30;

    /* §interaction — the warp spring + the gravity-well tunings (NUMERIC, NOT
       colors — read ONCE on mount via parseFloat, never reach a Canvas2D
       fillStyle, so immune to the W30 light-dark()/Canvas2D leak; AY.W-CON2).
       Mode-INVARIANT, declared ONCE in :root (the .dark arm needs no numeric
       re-declare — a per-mode value SPLIT is what proof:constellation-tokens
       clause (c) flags, and these tunings are identical in both modes).
       `--constellation-warp-response` is the keyframes.js ANGULAR PERIOD (the
       SwiftUI .spring(response:) axis), NOT a settle-duration — at ζ=1 the 2%
       settle lands at t₂ ≈ 5.83/ω₀ ≈ 0.93·response (the engine keeps the
       keyframes.js ω₀ = 2π/response convention, mints no second ω). */
    --constellation-warp-response:    0.55;   /* s — keyframes.js angular period (NOT a settle) */
    --constellation-warp-zeta:        1.0;    /* critically damped — a focal mark must NOT ring */
    --constellation-well-gain:        19000;  /* inverse-square force scale (the held-pull gain). Lifted 14000→19000 (BC.W-VIZ-CONSTELLATION): the GPU substrate's high-refresh frame loop ramps the held strength to only ~0.70 within the egg-live π perturb window, so the weaker effective force (fought by the |v|→speed ease-back) fell under the gate's ≥20% perturb floor — a stronger pull restores the heat. Held-physics only; rest is byte-untouched. Mirrors DEFAULT_WELL_CONFIG.gain. */
    --constellation-well-reach:       340;    /* base-width px — the well's reach (k-scaled at step) */
    --constellation-well-ramp:        8.0;    /* 1/s — the ARM rate. Doubled 4→8 (BC.W-VIZ-CONSTELLATION) so the strength reaches near-full power within the gate's 40-frame perturb window at the GPU substrate's high refresh (~0.12s bloom); release is the fixed brisk WELL_RELEASE_RAMP — the field-cools invariant, not consumer-tunable. Mirrors DEFAULT_WELL_CONFIG.ramp. */
    --constellation-well-max-speed:   4.0;    /* base-width px/frame cap — the no-slingshot clamp */
    --constellation-well-hold-ms:     140;    /* ms hold before the well arms */
    --constellation-wander-idle:      8000;   /* ms — min idle between auto re-targets (the W-CON1 drift cadence) */
    --constellation-wander-jitter:    8000;   /* ms — random extra idle per cadence (so the rhythm is not metronomic) */

    /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
       §6  SECTION PALETTE — 13-stop jewel tones (light)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ */
    /* AV.W16 TW2 — oklch ramp (perceptually-even L; hue preserved from the
       prior hsl() cadence). Categorical jewel tones — distinct hues, not a
       monotone scale. Authored as oklch pairs with the .dark mirror. */
    --section-color-0:  oklch(0.552 0.192 359.8);  /* rose       */
    --section-color-1:  oklch(0.502 0.165 305.9);  /* purple     */
    --section-color-2:  oklch(0.484 0.163 265.5);  /* indigo     */
    --section-color-3:  oklch(0.542 0.089 222.8);  /* teal-cyan  */
    --section-color-4:  oklch(0.551 0.088 171.1);  /* forest     */
    --section-color-5:  oklch(0.530 0.124 69.6);   /* amber — BC.W-ACCENT-TONE #13: the safeAccentColor-derived rebaseline (HEAD 0.623 → 0.530, 3.49:1 → 5.11:1 vs the warm-cream card; the dark arm already clears 8.07:1 so it stays). --viz-amber auto-tracks; fourier's :root override deletes on adopt. */
    --section-color-6:  oklch(0.579 0.201 30.4);   /* tomato-red */
    --section-color-7:  oklch(0.532 0.180 317.5);  /* violet     */
    --section-color-8:  oklch(0.520 0.176 8.4);    /* ruby       */
    --section-color-9:  oklch(0.492 0.038 239.6);  /* slate-blue */
    --section-color-10: oklch(0.556 0.103 128.8);  /* olive      */
    --section-color-11: oklch(0.601 0.092 208.0);  /* ocean      */
    --section-color-12: oklch(0.513 0.163 291.9);  /* periwinkle */

    /* Accent colors */
    --accent-pink: oklch(0.613 0.197 353.8);
    --section-heading: oklch(0.545 0.189 352.8);
    --accent-red: oklch(0.574 0.216 27.5);

    /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
       §6b VIZ BASIS — core brand hues
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ */
    --viz-fourier:   oklch(0.579 0.201 30.4);
    --viz-chebyshev: oklch(0.484 0.163 265.5);
    --viz-legendre:  oklch(0.532 0.180 317.5);
    --viz-amber:     var(--section-color-5);
    --viz-green:     var(--section-color-4);

    /* Semantic UI accents */
    --tier-featured: oklch(0.841 0.173 84.2);
    --tier-saved:    oklch(0.676 0.176 252.3);
    --like:          oklch(0.633 0.200 24.9);
    --success:       oklch(0.720 0.192 149.5);
    --warning:       oklch(0.770 0.165 70.6);
    --info:          oklch(0.626 0.186 259.6);
    --delete:        oklch(0.597 0.198 25.5);

    /* Semantic foregrounds — text/glyph atop --success/--warning/--info plates.
       Notification.vue currently hardcodes `text-white`/`text-foreground`
       inline; W2 migrates consumers to read these tokens. Warning carries a
       dark glyph because the amber plate is too luminous for white. R5
       vocab.γ gap row 4. */
    --success-foreground: var(--neutral-0);
    --warning-foreground: hsl(24 10% 10%);
    --info-foreground:    var(--neutral-0);

}

/* tokens/shadow.css
   §7 shadows — the cartoon offset-stamp signature + the shadow-color base.
   Carved from the tokens.css cascade root; @import-ed by tokens.css in
   cascade order. Same :root cascade context as its siblings. */
:root {
    /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
       §7  SHADOWS — cartoon offset is the signature
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ */
    /* BD.W-CARTOON-CEL-INK — the `--shadow-cartoon`/`-hover` aliases + the
       `--shadow-modal` cartoon plane re-point onto the warm `--cartoon-ink-lead`
       (0-blur down-LEFT, the cel family coherence — no neutral `--foreground` gray
       smudge). `--cartoon-ink-lead` is defined below in this same :root block (custom
       properties resolve order-independently). */
    --shadow-cartoon:       -3px 3px 0px 0px var(--cartoon-ink-lead);
    --shadow-cartoon-hover: -4px 4px 0px 0px var(--cartoon-ink-lead);
    --shadow-soft:          0 4px 12px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
    --shadow-elevated:      0 8px 24px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.12);
    --shadow-modal:
        -4px 4px 0px 0px var(--cartoon-ink-lead),
        0 24px 64px color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 12%, transparent);

    --cartoon-shadow:       var(--shadow-cartoon);
    --cartoon-shadow-hover: var(--shadow-cartoon-hover);
    --soft-shadow:          var(--shadow-soft);
    --elevated-shadow:      var(--shadow-elevated);
    --modal-shadow:         var(--shadow-modal);

    /* Elevation scale */
    --shadow-xs: 0 1px 3px color-mix(in srgb, var(--shadow-color) 4%, transparent),
        0 1px 2px color-mix(in srgb, var(--shadow-color) 6%, transparent);
    --shadow-sm: 0 2px 8px color-mix(in srgb, var(--shadow-color) 6%, transparent);
    --shadow-md: 0 4px 16px color-mix(in srgb, var(--shadow-color) 8%, transparent);
    --shadow-lg: 0 4px 20px color-mix(in srgb, var(--shadow-color) 12%, transparent);
    --shadow-xl: 0 8px 24px color-mix(in srgb, var(--shadow-color) 14%, transparent);
    --shadow-2xl: 0 25px 50px -12px color-mix(in srgb, var(--shadow-color) 25%, transparent);

    /* Z.W2.T3a — uniform-cast shadow rung. Offset 0 / 0, no directional
       Y bias. Use case: dock-hosted icons (e.g. the speedtest settings
       gear) where the surrounding dock shadow's downward cast reads as
       a per-icon right-edge halo on the rightmost child (A2 §B7). The
       uniform variant carries the same color-mix recipe family as the
       sized rungs (`--shadow-md`, `--shadow-lg`) so the perceived
       elevation is a peer, not a sibling. Consumers compose via
       `--shadow-dock-override: var(--shadow-uniform)` per-instance, or
       attach to per-icon-button stacks where directional cast is wrong-
       shape. Documented in DESIGN.md token table. */
    --shadow-uniform: 0 0 12px color-mix(in srgb, var(--shadow-color) 8%, transparent);

    /* Card = soft drop plate by default. The cartoon offset is a brand-flavoured
       decoration (a sticker look), not the canonical card shape. Consumers
       wanting the cartoon look pass <Card surface=\"cartoon\">, which composes the
       `.cartoon-surface` utility (it reads --shadow-cartoon directly). R3-spec
       §F.1, S.W4; Q.W3 Lane H. */
    --shadow-card:       var(--shadow-md);
    --card-shadow:       var(--shadow-card);

    /* AI.W1-α — sticky CardHeader background. Parameterises the
       `bg-card/60` literal value.js's PaneHeader.vue carried so the
       glass-ui canon owns the recipe. The scroll-driven `<CardHeader
       shrink>` choreography reads this token for its sticky-header
       backdrop tint; consumers retune per-app by overriding the
       variable at any ancestor. Auto-dark via `--card`. */
    --card-header-bg:    color-mix(in srgb, var(--card) 60%, transparent);

    /* Dock shadows — a single omnidirectional glow in the
       `--shadow-md`/`--shadow-lg` family (a directional drop concentrated on
       the pill's rounded right cap as a right-edge halo; the dock's own
       border already defines the silhouette). Per-instance directional drop
       reinstates via `--shadow-dock-override` (consumed by every `.glass-dock`
       state selector — see dock.css). */
    --shadow-dock:           0 0 20px color-mix(in srgb, var(--shadow-color) 14%, transparent);
    --shadow-dock-collapsed: 0 0 12px color-mix(in srgb, var(--shadow-color) 12%, transparent);
    --dock-shadow:           var(--shadow-dock);
    --dock-shadow-collapsed: var(--shadow-dock-collapsed);

    /* AX.W04 F1 — the wrap/card-tier dock shadow. A wrapped multi-row dock (and
       any finite-radius card silhouette) is a THICKER material than a flat thin
       pill — WWDC25 §219 \"material thickens as glass flexes larger\". So when the
       wrap dock expands it lifts its elevation onto the FLOATING-tier shadow
       stack (the `--glass-shadow-floating` family — `--shadow-xl` + the
       `--glass-material-rim` hairline + the `--glass-under-shadow-vivid` under-cast)
       rather than carrying the flat `--shadow-dock` glow of a 40px pill. Composed
       on `:root` as ONE consumer-overridable token (per the cartoon-shadow
       override contract — override on the cascade, never a dead local). The dock
       reads it via `calc()`-on-`--dock-expand-t` so the lift glides in lockstep
       with the pill→card radius morph (ONE clock, ONE register). */
    --glass-material-rim:    0 0 0 0.5px color-mix(in srgb, var(--shadow-color) 5%, transparent);
    --shadow-dock-wrap:      var(--shadow-xl),
        var(--glass-material-rim),
        var(--glass-under-shadow-vivid);

    /* Cartoon shadow color primitives (kept for tiered cartoon utilities) */
    --shadow-cartoon-color:             rgb(0 0 0 / 0.12);
    --shadow-cartoon-color-soft:        rgb(0 0 0 / 0.06);

    /* ── BD.W-CARTOON-CEL-INK — the cartoon register's OWN warm cel ink ──────────────
       Decoupled from `--shadow-color` so a consumer retunes the cast without
       disturbing every elevation box-shadow. Built by EXTRACTING the warm
       `--foreground` hue (`oklch from`) and FLOORING chroma (`max(c, 0.11)` —
       TECHNICOLOR, not merely-non-gray, the BA.W-NO-GRAY discipline applied to the
       cast) + DEEPENING L (`clamp(0.14, l, 0.18)`) so at stamp opacity it reads as a
       COLORED cel line, never a neutral gray. A cel cast is INK — it stays a warm DARK
       ink (the dark arm pins its own warm dark ink, dark-arm.css). The three planes
       step DOWN in opacity (lead 32% / mid 26% / contact 18%) — `in oklab` NOT
       `in srgb` (srgb muddies a warm dark ink toward gray as it dilutes; oklab holds
       the warm hue stable across the alpha ramp — the landed gray-dilution defect). */
    --cartoon-ink: oklch(from var(--foreground) clamp(0.14, l, 0.18) max(c, 0.11) h);
    --cartoon-ink-lead:    color-mix(in oklab, var(--cartoon-ink) 32%, transparent);
    --cartoon-ink-mid:     color-mix(in oklab, var(--cartoon-ink) 26%, transparent);
    --cartoon-ink-contact: color-mix(in oklab, var(--cartoon-ink) 18%, transparent);
    /* `@supports not (oklch from …)` floors the ink to a named warm sRGB literal
       (a legacy engine gets a WARM — not gray — stamp; NO dead inline literal). */
    --cartoon-ink-fallback: #4a3320;

    /* BD.W-CARTOON-CEL-INK — the tiered cel-ink STAMP, re-pointed to the warm
       `--cartoon-ink` rungs at HARD 0-BLUR (lead + mid + contact all 0px — the cel
       edge; the landed 1–2px soft blur is the gray-smudge defect), keeping the LANDED
       down-LEFT geometry (REFINE-not-flip — the direction is coherent with the
       upper-right `--glass-key`; cast lands lower-LEFT). φ-cadenced offsets. */
    --shadow-cartoon-sm:
        -2px 2px 0 var(--cartoon-ink-lead),
        -3px 3px 0 var(--cartoon-ink-mid),
        -4px 4px 0 var(--cartoon-ink-contact);
    --shadow-cartoon-md:
        -3px 3px 0 var(--cartoon-ink-lead),
        -5px 5px 0 var(--cartoon-ink-mid),
        -7px 7px 0 var(--cartoon-ink-contact);
    --shadow-cartoon-lg:
        -4px 4px 0 var(--cartoon-ink-lead),
        -7px 7px 0 var(--cartoon-ink-mid),
       -11px 11px 0 var(--cartoon-ink-contact);
    --cartoon-shadow-sm: var(--shadow-cartoon-sm);
    --cartoon-shadow-md: var(--shadow-cartoon-md);
    --cartoon-shadow-lg: var(--shadow-cartoon-lg);

    /* AY.W-COHERE E2 — the gel-bead ambient contact shadow (the GooBlob lit dome).
       The metaball bead is NOT a Memphis sticker — it is a lit gel dome sitting IN
       a scene, so it casts a SOFT, NEAR-CENTERED, low-offset ambient contact
       shadow (small downward offset, a large soft blur, ambient-tinted), in the
       same soft-ambient-light register the constellation / fourier / aurora
       substrates speak — NOT the hard `5px 5px` near-black offset-stamp the
       cartoon family carries (that stamp stays the identity of
       <Card surface=\"cartoon\">; it is removed ONLY from the gel bead, a different
       lighting language). The shadow color is ambient: the bead's own
       `--blob-color` mixed toward `--shadow-color` at a LOW strength, so the dome
       casts a faint tinted contact shade. Adaptive-by-construction: `--shadow-color`
       is `var(--foreground)`, which flips light→dark, so the contact shadow
       re-resolves under `.dark` with NO hardcoded `.dark` re-declaration (the house
       cartoon-shadow contract's pattern, applied to the ambient shadow). A consumer
       retunes the bead's lighting by overriding `--blob-shadow` (or the
       `--blob-shadow-*` sub-axes) on the cascade. */
    --blob-shadow-offset: 0 4px;
    --blob-shadow-blur:   14px;
    --blob-shadow-color:  color-mix(in srgb,
        var(--blob-color, var(--shadow-color)) 22%, var(--shadow-color));
    --blob-shadow-strength: 20%;
    /* The AMBIENT cast (the soft diffuse drop) — the dome floats a little above its
       surface. This is the first drop-shadow rung. */
    --blob-shadow-ambient: var(--blob-shadow-offset) var(--blob-shadow-blur)
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--blob-shadow-color) var(--blob-shadow-strength), transparent);

    /* AZ.W-BLOB-STUDIO D4 — the GROUNDED gel-dome read. A single soft ambient cast
       reads as a swatch FLOATING — there is no contact, no AO, nothing that says the
       dome SITS on a surface. The grounded read is a TWO-RUNG drop-shadow composite
       (drop-shadow filters chain, and EACH follows the irregular metaball silhouette —
       a box-shadow would stamp a rectangle, missing the necking satellites): the soft
       AMBIENT cast above PLUS a TIGHT, LOW-OFFSET, DARKER CONTACT band hugging the dome
       base. The contact rung is the gravity cue — a near-zero blur, a small downward
       offset, and ~2× the ambient strength, so the silhouette base READS pressed against
       the surface (the contact-darkening AO the satellites pick up where they merge into
       the body, since the drop-shadow hugs the WHOLE necked silhouette including the
       satellite bulges). Both rungs ride the SAME `--blob-shadow-color` (the ambient
       `--shadow-color`/`--foreground` base), so the grounded read re-resolves under
       `.dark` with no hardcoded dark block (the house cartoon-shadow adaptive pattern).
       A consumer retunes grounding by overriding `--blob-shadow-contact-*` or the whole
       `--blob-shadow` composite on the cascade. */
    --blob-shadow-contact-offset: 0 1px;
    --blob-shadow-contact-blur:   3px;
    --blob-shadow-contact-strength: 38%;
    --blob-shadow-contact: var(--blob-shadow-contact-offset) var(--blob-shadow-contact-blur)
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--blob-shadow-color) var(--blob-shadow-contact-strength), transparent);

    /* The grounded composite is consumed by GooBlob.vue as TWO chained drop-shadow()
       filters: drop-shadow(ambient) drop-shadow(contact). `--blob-shadow` keeps the
       single-rung ambient value (back-compat: a consumer reading the old token still
       gets the soft cast); the grounded read is the explicit two-rung pair. */
    --blob-shadow: var(--blob-shadow-ambient);

    /* The hover lift — the ambient cast deepens + softens (the dome rises) AND the
       contact band TIGHTENS toward the lifted base (a touch of extra grounding-darken
       as the dome presses on its surface on the rise). */
    --blob-shadow-hover: 0 6px 20px
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--blob-shadow-color) 26%, transparent);
    --blob-shadow-contact-hover: 0 1px 4px
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--blob-shadow-color) 44%, transparent);

}

/* BD.W-CARTOON-CEL-INK — the relative-color-syntax fallback. On a legacy engine that
   lacks `oklch(from …)`, `--cartoon-ink` would be invalid (the whole cast drops). Floor
   it to the named warm sRGB literal `--cartoon-ink-fallback` (NOT a dead inline literal)
   so the cel stamp degrades to a WARM — never gray, never empty — ink. The three
   strength rungs re-derive off the floored ink for free (they `color-mix` it). */
@supports not (color: oklch(from white l c h)) {
    :root {
        --cartoon-ink: var(--cartoon-ink-fallback);
    }
}

/* BD.W-CARTOON-CEL-INK — prefers-contrast / reduced-transparency floor the inked edge
   UP (the cel cast is a legibility ASSET, not decoration; PRT is NOT a no-op — the cast
   is translucent ink). */
@media (prefers-contrast: more) {
    :root {
        --cartoon-ink-lead:    color-mix(in oklab, var(--cartoon-ink) 42%, transparent);
        --cartoon-ink-mid:     color-mix(in oklab, var(--cartoon-ink) 34%, transparent);
        --cartoon-ink-contact: color-mix(in oklab, var(--cartoon-ink) 24%, transparent);
    }
}
@media (prefers-reduced-transparency: reduce) {
    :root {
        --cartoon-ink-lead:    color-mix(in oklab, var(--cartoon-ink) 42%, transparent);
        --cartoon-ink-mid:     color-mix(in oklab, var(--cartoon-ink) 34%, transparent);
        --cartoon-ink-contact: color-mix(in oklab, var(--cartoon-ink) 24%, transparent);
    }
}

/* tokens/glass.css
   §8 glassmorphism — the five-tier surface ladder (opacity/blur/saturate rungs +
   composed bg/border) + the --glass-level axis + the control-REST register.
   Carved from the tokens.css cascade root; @import-ed by tokens.css in
   cascade order. Same :root cascade context as its siblings. The decorative/fx
   tail (grain/specular/edge-light + the adaptive tint-source/backdrop-luma bucket +
   fringe/curvature/chart-palette + the per-tier shadow/spine/under-shadow/overlay-
   scrim) carved to tokens/glass-fx.css at BB.W-CARVE4 — @import-ed IMMEDIATELY AFTER
   this partial so the two adjacent same-selector :root{} blocks resolve identically
   (the dist is byte-isomorphic). */
:root {
    /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
       §8  GLASSMORPHISM — Five-tier surface ladder (v0.8.0)

       Tier semantics, alpha-monotonic:
         wash      ~0.30α  + light blur      (was `subtle`)
         quiet     ~0.50α  + medium blur     (the formerly missing rung)
         resting   ~0.65α  + canonical blur  (was `default`; the speedtest plate)
         floating  ~0.80α  + heavier blur    (was `elevated`)
         overlay   ~0.95α  + heaviest blur   (NEW; modal-over-modal)

       v0.8.0 retires the four-tier `subtle | default | medium | elevated`
       ladder outright. No legacy aliases. Consumers migrate at the same
       breaking change. R3-spec §A.
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ */

    /* ── Per-tier alpha — the CANONICAL named opacity register (BB.W-CARD-TIER-ALPHA) ──
       Each `--glass-opacity-{tier}` IS the library's canonical alpha for that tier —
       a NAMED, documented input to the ONE compose recipe below, NOT a magic number
       a consumer re-pins. The values here are the library IDENTITY: the warm-cream
       glass ladder, alpha-monotonic (wash < quiet < resting < floating < overlay), with
       the dock (0.42) + chassis (0.28) carrying their own footprint-calibrated rungs.
       The dark arm (tokens/dark-arm.css) lifts each ~0.08 for read-weight over the
       deeper canvas — the SAME named tokens, per-mode (the plain per-mode pair idiom).

       A consumer that wants the LIBRARY register reads these named tokens (or composes
       a `<Card tier>` rung) and re-pins NOTHING; a consumer whose surface needs a
       divergent composited luminance (the speedtest dark-AA register: instrument 0.72 /
       document 0.78) keeps that as a NAMED preset IN ITS OWN repo (presets-in-consumers)
       — the library default is its identity, the consumer preset is the consumer's.

       THE SUBSTITUTION-VS-INHERITANCE SEAM (the recurring trap, recorded). The composed
       `--glass-bg-{tier}` below is declared HERE at `:root`, so a custom-property
       substitution resolves at THIS element — overriding `--glass-opacity-{tier}` on a
       DESCENDANT does NOT re-compose the inherited `--glass-bg-{tier}` (the value is
       already resolved). A consumer that needs a per-scope tier alpha therefore RE-DECLARES
       the composed `--glass-bg-{tier}` on the scope (the same recipe, the pinned alpha) —
       the documented retune path, mirroring the dock's own `--glass-bg-dock` self-re-point.
       The named alpha is the canonical INPUT; the per-scope `--glass-bg-*` re-declaration is
       the sanctioned consumer pin. (The `--glass-level` recipe MECHANISM below is the
       AX.W54 seam — UNTOUCHED by this register; this canonicalizes the alpha INPUTS only.)
       Machine-locked by `proof:card-tier-alpha`. */
    /* Opacity primitives — the per-tier canonical alpha (light arm). */
    --glass-opacity-wash:     0.30;
    --glass-opacity-quiet:    0.50;
    --glass-opacity-resting:  0.65;
    --glass-opacity-floating: 0.80;
    --glass-opacity-overlay:  0.95;

    /* Blur primitives. The radii here feather the dock + meter card to a
       barely-perceptible diffusion that lets the substrate behind it bleed
       through cleanly while keeping hero typography crisp at meter scale.
       The resting blur sits at 10px so a glass plate reads as a diffusion
       without consumer-side override.

       BA.W-GLASS-CAL Unit 1 — the ladder dialed back ~15-20% UNIFORMLY (the
       user's \"a hair too much\" over-diffusion): quiet 10→8, resting 12→10,
       floating 16→13, overlay 15→13, dock 11→9; wash stays 1px (sub-perceptual).
       The pull is proportional (it preserves tier separation) and keeps every
       rung in the AV.W7-F2 8-15px band — the backdrop's structure reads a hair
       more through the plate while the glass still reads unmistakably as glass.
       The radius axis ONLY: `--glass-level` + every `saturate()`/`brightness()`
       companion below are UNTOUCHED (the W-DARK-MATERIAL dark-recipe saturate/
       brightness companions preserved — radius-only, the gate's B3 assert). */
    --glass-blur-wash-radius:     1px;
    --glass-blur-quiet-radius:    8px;
    --glass-blur-resting-radius:  10px;
    --glass-blur-floating-radius: 13px;
    /* AV.W7 F2 — clamped from 24px into the 8–15px budget band. A blur radius
       past ~20px is exponentially costlier (`backdrop-filter` is glass-ui's most
       expensive idiom — the radius is the dominant cost). 15px is the band
       ceiling; BA.W-GLASS-CAL dialed the overlay rung to 13px (off 15) so the
       whole ladder pulls back in proportion. A high-resolution display restores
       a richer wash via the @media arm in light-dark.css — the cost is amortised
       by the extra device pixels. */
    --glass-blur-overlay-radius:  13px;
    /* Dock blur radius — 9px (BA.W-GLASS-CAL, off 11), paired with the 0.42
       opacity below: a crisp glass pill (the iOS Control Center register), not a
       feathered wash. The dock composes `blur()` only (no saturate); the
       `prefers-reduced-transparency` bracket at glass.css maps it to the
       opacity-only register. */
    --glass-blur-dock-radius:     9px;

    /* ── Per-rung SATURATE — the CANONICAL named saturate register (BC.W-GLASS-
       LEGIBILITY-MEASURED, the speedtest-AX BC-W10 fold) ───────────────────────
       The saturate term is the LOAD-BEARING glass knob (research/apple-glass.md §4:
       the apple.com nav rides `saturate(1.8)` — the highest term, blur moderate).
       Each `--glass-saturate-{tier}` is the library's canonical saturation for that
       rung — a NAMED, `:root`-overridable input the composed `--glass-blur-{tier}`
       reads THROUGH, the SAME substitution-vs-redeclaration discipline as the
       `--glass-opacity-{tier}` alpha register above. A `:root { --glass-saturate-
       quiet: … }` override re-resolves the rung's saturation with ZERO recipe edit
       (the speedtest \"dark cards\" chronic + the apple-glass saturate-as-load-bearing
       lever). The values here ARE the W52-D19 bake (wash/quiet/resting 1.05,
       floating 1.18, overlay 1.2) become the token defaults — so the composed blur
       is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the old baked literal (proof:glass-cal / proof:card-tier-
       alpha stay GREEN by construction). The deep-tier `--glass-saturate-deep` 1.8
       ceiling (glass-deep.css) is the upper bound; no rung saturate exceeds it. The
       dark arm (tokens/dark-arm.css) re-declares this register with the W-DARK-
       MATERIAL luminosity lift — the SAME named tokens, per-mode (the plain per-mode
       pair idiom). The brightness() companions stay baked (this fold mints ONLY the
       saturate knob). Machine-locked by `proof:glass-legibility` L7. */
    --glass-saturate-wash:     1.4;
    --glass-saturate-quiet:    1.4;
    --glass-saturate-resting:  1.4;
    --glass-saturate-floating: 1.6;
    --glass-saturate-overlay:  1.6;
    /* W-NAV-DOCK-FIX — the dock's named light-concentration saturate. The dock is a
       calm chrome strip (the wash/quiet/resting register 1.4, NOT the floating 1.6).
       Mirrors the dark arm's dock saturate; the single retune knob in both modes. The
       light dock was the ONLY light surface with no saturate companion — over the flat
       warm-cream page the un-saturated backdrop-filter pulled the cream toward neutral
       (the flat-slab gray read). */
    --glass-saturate-dock:     1.4;

    /* AX.W54 — each blur radius is scaled by the ONE `--glass-level` scalar (in
       lockstep with the opacity seam above): level=1 byte-identical; level=0 →
       blur(0) (the clean solid floor with the opacity); level>1 → more diffusion.
       The per-rung `saturate()` companion reads the named `--glass-saturate-{tier}`
       knob (BC.W-GLASS-LEGIBILITY-MEASURED); `brightness()` stays baked (the level
       scales the RADIUS, not the saturation — W52's calm light-concentration). */
    --glass-blur-wash:     blur(calc(var(--glass-blur-wash-radius) * var(--glass-level))) saturate(var(--glass-saturate-wash));
    /* AV.W15 M1 — the `quiet` rung gained `saturate(1.05) brightness(1.02)`
       so the lower ladder reads with the same saturation life as `resting`+
       (Apple: Liquid Glass concentrates light, it does not desaturate). The
       `brightness(1.02)` is the small lift the spec names — it lands `quiet`
       between bare `wash` and `resting` without over-brightening the rung.
       The `prefers-reduced-transparency: reduce` bracket at glass.css maps
       `--glass-blur-quiet: none`, so this whole chain drops with the blur. */
    --glass-blur-quiet:    blur(calc(var(--glass-blur-quiet-radius) * var(--glass-level))) saturate(var(--glass-saturate-quiet)) brightness(1.02);
    --glass-blur-resting:  blur(calc(var(--glass-blur-resting-radius) * var(--glass-level))) saturate(var(--glass-saturate-resting));
    /* AX.W52 D19 — the floating/overlay backdrop saturate tamed from 1.4/1.5 to a
       gentle ~1.18/1.2 (real liquid glass concentrates light ~1.1–1.2×; the prior
       1.4–1.5× over-juiced the bleed-through into a garish smear over a colored
       substrate). wash/quiet/resting already sit at 1.05 — untouched. */
    --glass-blur-floating: blur(calc(var(--glass-blur-floating-radius) * var(--glass-level))) saturate(var(--glass-saturate-floating));
    --glass-blur-overlay:  blur(calc(var(--glass-blur-overlay-radius) * var(--glass-level))) saturate(var(--glass-saturate-overlay));
    /* Dock blur composes blur() only — no saturate. */
    /* W-NAV-DOCK-FIX — the dock concentrates light like every other tier (apple-glass
       §4): the saturate lifts the warm-cream chroma THROUGH the plate, the small
       brightness is the light-concentration the quiet rung carries. The blur RADIUS is
       untouched (the W-GLASS-CAL dial). Compositor-only (backdrop-filter is a paint op);
       the prefers-reduced-transparency bracket maps --glass-blur-dock: none, so the
       saturate drops with the blur (no separate carve owed). */
    --glass-blur-dock:
        blur(calc(var(--glass-blur-dock-radius) * var(--glass-level)))
        saturate(var(--glass-saturate-dock))
        brightness(1.02);

    /* AX.W52 → BC.W-GLASS-LEGIBILITY-MEASURED — the GLASS BUTTON blur, lifted to
       the FLOATING-tier \"more glass\" register. The `glass`/`glass-wash` button
       variants compose the `.glass-wash` tier (`--glass-blur-wash` = blur(1px)) — a
       sub-perceptual diffusion authored for a small detail TILE, not a button the
       user reads as \"glass.\" A button is a hero control: it wants a real glass blur.
       The AX.W52 landing sat at the quiet-tier 8px/saturate(1.05) — \"quieter than
       even the calm floating tier\" (apple-ios27.md §4.2). This wave lifts it to the
       FLOATING-tier register (apple-ios27.md §5 bake-table \"button blur lift to
       10-13 / 1.18\"): the floating radius (13px, in the 10-13 band) + the named
       `--glass-saturate-floating` knob (1.18) — a genuinely MORE-glass transmissive
       capsule revealing the craft beneath (the apple-glass SUPERSET: apple.com's web
       CTA is a SOLID blue capsule, zero blur — research/apple-glass.md §1.4). The
       `brightness(1.02)` light-concentration the quiet rung carried is KEPT (no
       colour-juice). Clean break — the old 8px/quiet value is GONE, no alias. The
       wash TILE tier (MetricCell et al.) keeps its 1px — only the glass BUTTON
       variants reach for this. Machine-locked by `proof:glass-legibility` L3. */
    --glass-blur-btn:      blur(calc(var(--glass-blur-floating-radius) * var(--glass-level))) saturate(var(--glass-saturate-floating)) brightness(1.02);

    /* Dock-specific opacity (0.42) — lighter than `resting` (0.65) so the dock
       reads as a translucent overlay rather than a panel, backdrop bleed
       visible through it. Consumed by `.glass-dock` via `--glass-bg-dock`. The
       value holds the surface register against the tightened 11px blur so the
       dock reads as painted chrome, not a frosted ghost. */
    /* BD.W-DOCK-CORE (D3) — the dock base alpha lifts 0.42 → 0.50 (margin-insurance for
       the warm-chromatic darken: the base composite chroma closes a notch before any tint
       engages). Stays the lightest chrome tier (below resting 0.65), mode-invariant. */
    --glass-opacity-dock: 0.50;

    /* Dialog-specific opacity (0.68) — BC.W-DIALOG-GLASS. The iOS-27 control-
       center modal register: SEE-THROUGH frosted glass that reveals the page
       beneath, NOT the floating-tier near-opaque slab (0.80 read as \"NOT glassy
       at all\"). 0.68 sits between `resting` (0.65 — transparent enough to read
       through) and `floating` (0.80 — opaque enough for AA legibility against
       the bright bucket), so the modal floats with the FLOATING-tier lift (edge/
       rim/under-shadow) while reading at the RESTING-tier transparency. The
       LIBRARY iOS-modal identity (presets-in-consumers — a consumer retunes
       `--glass-bg-dialog` via :root). Consumed by DialogContent.vue, which re-
       declares `--glass-bg-floating: var(--glass-bg-dialog)` on the dialog scope
       (the self-re-point recipe, the dock precedent — NOT a raw rung override
       that won't re-compose). */
    --glass-opacity-dialog: 0.68;

    /* Sheet-specific opacity (0.74) — BE.W-SHEET-TRANSLUCENT. The iOS-27 bottom-
       sheet register: SEE-THROUGH frosted glass whose lifted crown reveals the
       page/album grid beneath, NOT the near-opaque overlay slab (0.95 read as \"not
       glassy\"). 0.74 sits BETWEEN the dialog 0.68 (transparent enough that the
       backdrop reads through) and the overlay 0.95 (opaque enough for AA body text
       against a busy grid) — a sheet is content-bearing, so it sits a hair above the
       dialog. The composed `--glass-bg-sheet` rides the SAME oklab-tint-wrapped
       recipe `--glass-bg-dialog` speaks, so the W55 bright-bucket darken + the
       `--glass-level` opaque escape + the dark-arm saturate companions reach the
       sheet with ZERO new compositing seam. Drawer + Sheet self-re-point onto it.
       Presets-in-consumers — a consumer retunes `--glass-opacity-sheet` via :root. */
    --glass-opacity-sheet: 0.74;

    /* Clear-specific opacity (0.58) — BE.W-CLEAR-VARIANT. The Apple-Clear MAXIMALLY
       translucent register — the clearest rung the library ships, BELOW the dialog
       0.68 (the now-playing pill, the Control-Center clear tile: the album grid
       bleeds genuinely through). It is STRUCTURALLY coupled to a MANDATORY legibility
       scrim (the `surface=\"clear\"` decoration carries a `::before` backdrop-dim whose
       strength derives from the sampled `--glass-backdrop-luma`) — a scrim-less clear
       plate is FORBIDDEN by construction (the Apple Clear contract: near-transparent →
       the text needs a scrim floor). 0.58 sits in the Apple-Clear band (clearer than
       the modal 0.68, still a plate not a ghost — > 0.45). The composed
       `--glass-bg-clear` rides the SAME oklab-tint-wrapped recipe so the W55 darken +
       the `--glass-level` escape reach it. Presets-in-consumers — retune via :root. */
    --glass-opacity-clear: 0.58;

    /* Dock foreground over an animated backdrop. A dock floating over an
       aurora/animated surface (`value.js` dock-over-aurora, a slides routed
       control, bbnf) sits on a mid-luminance, shifting field where the muted
       default fg can drop below contrast. This token is the dock control's
       foreground base — a complete `hsl()` color consumed via `var()`; for the
       muted icon weight the control mixes it down with `--opacity-icon-muted`.
       Defaults to `var(--foreground)` so the dock paints byte-identically to
       today; a consumer overrides it per-backdrop (e.g. a near-white over a
       dark aurora) without touching library source.

       AX.W55 — this is the FOREGROUND half of the adaptive story, now reconciled
       INTO the `--glass-backdrop` probe (not a third fork): under the bright bucket
       (`@container style(--glass-backdrop: light)` in dock.css) it re-points toward
       the contrast-clearing ink, and an `@supports (color: contrast-color(…))`
       block flips it natively on supporting engines. The `var(--foreground)`
       default keeps the zero-delta rest state. */
    --dock-fg-on-aurora: var(--foreground);

    /* Chassis-specific opacity. Looser than `default` (0.50) so the
       <InstrumentChassis> surface reads as glass-over-aurora at its
       64rem-wide footprint; the default tier stays calibrated for cards.
       Mirrors the dock-tier pattern (own opacity primitive + composed bg).
       Light 0.28 sits in the gap between dock (0.32) and a hypothetical
       wash tier; dark 0.36 mirrors the +0.08 step the dock takes from
       `glass-bg-medium` (0.65) down to its own 0.32. */
    --glass-opacity-chassis: 0.28;

    /* Composed backgrounds — AX.W54: the opacity is threaded through the ONE
       `--glass-level` clarity scalar. Each rung's effective alpha is
       `1 - (1 - <rung-opacity>) * --glass-level`: at level=1 it collapses to the
       hand-tuned `<rung-opacity>` (byte-identical); at level=0 it resolves `1`
       (solid `--card` — the opaque escape); at level>1 it goes clearer. ONE
       expression, the same seven rungs. */
    --glass-bg-wash:     color-mix(in srgb, var(--card) calc((1 - (1 - var(--glass-opacity-wash)) * var(--glass-level)) * 100%), transparent);
    --glass-bg-quiet:    color-mix(in srgb, var(--card) calc((1 - (1 - var(--glass-opacity-quiet)) * var(--glass-level)) * 100%), transparent);
    --glass-bg-resting:  color-mix(in srgb, var(--card) calc((1 - (1 - var(--glass-opacity-resting)) * var(--glass-level)) * 100%), transparent);
    --glass-bg-floating: color-mix(in srgb, var(--card) calc((1 - (1 - var(--glass-opacity-floating)) * var(--glass-level)) * 100%), transparent);
    --glass-bg-overlay:  color-mix(in srgb, var(--card) calc((1 - (1 - var(--glass-opacity-overlay)) * var(--glass-level)) * 100%), transparent);
    /* The dock bg ALSO rides the oklab tint seam (AX.W54 amendment): the same
       `color-mix(in oklab, …, --glass-tint-source --glass-tint-strength)` wrapper
       the rungs reach for, ZERO-delta at `--glass-tint-strength: 0%` — so W55's
       adaptive darken can reach the dock (the G2 over-light surface) without a
       second hand-rolled recipe. */
    --glass-bg-dock:     color-mix(in oklab, color-mix(in srgb, var(--card) calc((1 - (1 - var(--glass-opacity-dock)) * var(--glass-level)) * 100%), transparent), var(--glass-tint-source) var(--glass-tint-strength));
    --glass-bg-chassis:  color-mix(in srgb, var(--card) calc((1 - (1 - var(--glass-opacity-chassis)) * var(--glass-level)) * 100%), transparent);
    /* BC.W-DIALOG-GLASS — the dialog plate bg, composed the SAME oklab-tint-wrapped
       way the dock self-re-points (so the BC.W-ADAPTIVE-RECONCILE darken reaches the
       modal over a bright backdrop without a second hand-rolled recipe). The 0.68
       opacity is the iOS-27 control-center register (see `--glass-opacity-dialog`).
       DialogContent.vue re-declares `--glass-bg-floating: var(--glass-bg-dialog)` on
       the dialog scope (the documented retune path — the self-re-point, NOT a raw
       rung override) so the floating edge/rim/under-shadow LIFT survives the
       transparency drop. */
    --glass-bg-dialog:   color-mix(in oklab, color-mix(in srgb, var(--card) calc((1 - (1 - var(--glass-opacity-dialog)) * var(--glass-level)) * 100%), transparent), var(--glass-tint-source) var(--glass-tint-strength));
    /* BE.W-SHEET-TRANSLUCENT — the bottom-sheet plate bg, composed the SAME oklab-
       tint-wrapped way the dialog self-re-points (so the W55 darken + the dark-arm
       saturate companion reach the see-through sheet without a second hand-rolled
       recipe — byte-isomorphic to `--glass-bg-dialog`, only the opacity token
       differs). Drawer + SheetContent re-declare `--glass-bg-floating:
       var(--glass-bg-sheet)` on their scope (the self-re-point recipe — NOT a raw
       rung override that won't re-compose) so the floating edge/rim/under-shadow LIFT
       survives the transparency drop. */
    --glass-bg-sheet:    color-mix(in oklab, color-mix(in srgb, var(--card) calc((1 - (1 - var(--glass-opacity-sheet)) * var(--glass-level)) * 100%), transparent), var(--glass-tint-source) var(--glass-tint-strength));
    /* BE.W-CLEAR-VARIANT — the maximally-translucent Apple-Clear plate bg, composed
       the SAME oklab-tint-wrapped way (so the W55 darken reaches the clear plate).
       The `surface=\"clear\"` decoration reads this fill AND couples its MANDATORY
       `::before` legibility scrim (the scrim dims the BACKDROP — distinct from this
       PLATE tint; the two axes stay disjoint). Byte-isomorphic to `--glass-bg-dialog`,
       only the (lower) clear opacity differs. */
    --glass-bg-clear:    color-mix(in oklab, color-mix(in srgb, var(--card) calc((1 - (1 - var(--glass-opacity-clear)) * var(--glass-level)) * 100%), transparent), var(--glass-tint-source) var(--glass-tint-strength));

    /* ── BE.W-TINTED-CHIP — the per-INSTANCE PLATE-FILL tint axis (`--glass-fill-tint`
       / `--glass-fill-strength`) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
       The THIRD per-instance chromatic axis (BESIDE the rim `--glass-accent` and the
       whole-plate legibility `--glass-tint-source`): `--glass-fill-tint` tints the
       plate BACKGROUND BODY toward a consumer DATA hue, so a glass surface reads as
       COLORED GLASS (a green chip, a violet facet) without re-declaring the composed
       `--glass-bg-*` token. DISTINCT-not-fork of `--glass-accent` (which tints only the
       RIM + the `::before` specular CORE — file-line-disjoint): the fill writes the
       plate body, the accent writes the rim; a chip may carry BOTH. The mix is `in
       oklab` (the perceptual glass-tint family — NOT the in-srgb `--surface-tint-*`
       brand-overlay fence, AW.W26). It rides ON TOP of the W55 legibility composite
       (the plate still darkens-over-light for AA AND carries the data hue — additive,
       never a replacement). At the `@property` defaults (transparent + 0%) the body is
       byte-identical to today (`color-mix(in oklab, X, transparent 0%)` ≡ X — the no-op
       floor). `<GlassChip :tone>` is the first-class consumer (BE.W-TINTED-CHIP
       component) — the fill is read on the surface, never written into a `--glass-bg-*`
       token here. The `@property` registration is below (the typed no-op floor). */

    /* Composed borders — foreground-tinted. BC.W-BLACK-BAR D2 — the perimeter
       hairline is RETIRED as the silhouette device. The old 11-22% warm-ink rung
       borders rode --foreground (oklab L 0.216, near-black warm ink); over a
       near-white cream plate the full-perimeter hairline read as the user's \"wtf
       is this black bar\" — worst on the TOP edge where the bright white catch-light
       ring sat just inside a dark border (a bright rim and a dark line at war on the
       SAME edge). The silhouette is now carved by the directional rim
       (--glass-rim-top catch-light + --glass-rim-bottom under-shadow, glass-fx.css),
       NOT by this perimeter ink. So each rung drops to ≤5% α — the warm hue stays
       --foreground (the warm-not-tinted identity, BA.W-NO-GRAY), only the PRESENCE
       falls below the read-threshold so the hairline can never read as a bar. The
       +1% on the two heaviest rungs (floating/overlay) holds a faint tier-presence
       ordering without crossing the threshold (apple-ios27.md §5 \"top ≤4%\" target). */
    --glass-border-wash:     color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 4%, transparent);
    --glass-border-quiet:    color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 4%, transparent);
    --glass-border-resting:  color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 4%, transparent);
    --glass-border-floating: color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 5%, transparent);
    --glass-border-overlay:  color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 5%, transparent);
    /* W-NAV-DOCK-FIX — the dock is FLOATING chrome over an UNKNOWN backdrop (unlike a
       content card on the page), so it earns a readable warm-INK hairline (the iOS-27
       edge-rim layer). 8% warm-ink is still a whisper, never a black bar (--foreground
       H≈56° → a WARM rim, never a gray line). */
    --glass-border-dock:     color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 8%, transparent);
    --glass-border:          var(--glass-border-quiet);

    /* ── The control REST register (BA.W-SURFACE-AXIS scope 7) ────────────────
       ONE shared resting surface for the FORM family — Input/Textarea/Select/
       Combobox/NumberField/TagsInput — so the whole control band is ONE material
       at rest, not a per-control fork. Input rode the warm near-white `quiet`
       glass register while Select rode the lighter `glass-wash` gray (live-probed
       at R10 SM-3/NF-4: input `oklab(0.986)` near-white vs select `oklab(0.636)`
       gray — the \"no gray\" bar at a control-family seam). The unified register
       adopts the WARMER input rung (the no-gray choice); the per-control recipes
       re-point to read these tokens, no per-control fork. The W-NO-GRAY warm floor
       lands underneath in Batch 1b (it warms the VALUES); this register unifies
       the TIER. The hover lift mixes the rest fill one step toward the resting
       rung so a control brightens on hover in lockstep. */
    --control-surface-bg:        var(--glass-bg-quiet);
    --control-surface-border:    var(--glass-border-floating);
    --control-surface-blur:      var(--glass-blur-quiet);
    --control-surface-bg-hover:  color-mix(in oklab, var(--glass-bg-quiet), var(--glass-bg-resting) 35%);

    /* The decorative/fx tail (grain/specular/edge-light + the adaptive tint-source/
       backdrop-luma bucket + fringe/curvature/chart-palette + per-tier shadow/spine/
       under-shadow/overlay-scrim) lives in tokens/glass-fx.css (carved at BB.W-CARVE4
       — the next adjacent :root{} block in the cascade). */
}

